    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/associate-professor-at-the-department-of-public-health": {
      "title": "Associate Professor",
      "description": "Associate Professor at the Department of Public Health - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU Danish website You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability Campus development History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Public Health - Faculty: Health Sciences Associate Professor at the Department of Public Health Deadline 20 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 May Department of Public Health Bartholins Allé 2 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 168122 Apply The Department of Public Health at the Faculty of Health, Aarhus University, invites applications for one or more full-time, permanent positions as Associate Professor within one or more of our main academic areas: Applied Public Health, Clinical Public Health, Epidemiology, Environment & Biostatistics, and Sport Science. The teaching profile for the position is within philosophy of science. The expected start date is 1 May 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. About the Department The Department of Public Health is a research-intensive environment dedicated to advancing fundamental scientific knowledge and developing applied solutions to major public health challenges. Our research spans theoretical, basic, and applied sciences, with the shared ambition to generate impactful knowledge and improving population health. Academic excellence is at the core of everything we do. We are committed to conducting rigorous and innovative research and delivering high-quality teaching. The department is responsible for teaching in public health, sport science, health science, nursing science, and related methodological disciplines, as well as delivering a substantial proportion of the teaching within the medical degree programme. The successful candidate will be expected to teach philosophy of science across these programmes. While firmly rooted in our respective disciplines, we actively foster interdisciplinary collaboration to advance innovation and excellence in both research and education. We are equally committed to cultivating a healthy, inclusive, and supportive workplace culture characterised by open dialogue, mutual respect, and an informal atmosphere. We believe that academic excellence and personal well-being are mutually reinforcing and essential to long-term success. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Your job responsibilities As an Associate Professor, you will contribute actively to the department's research and teaching activities. You are expected to conduct research at a high international level and to develop an independent academic profile through both individual scholarship and collaboration with colleagues. You are committed to delivering high-quality teaching and supervision and bring strong collaborative skills, intellectual curiosity, and a willingness to contribute broadly across the educational programmes and courses for which the department is responsible. Your primary teaching responsibilities will be within the areas of philosophy of science across a range of degree programme. You are eager to engage in the academic community at Aarhus University and contribute to strengthening the department's national and international profile. You are committed to maintaining high academic and pedagogical standards in your teaching, and you bring strong collaborative skills and a broad academic network that you actively leverage to strengthen the Department of Public Health’s academic profile, nationally and internationally. We value academic citizenship. This includes active participation in collegial collaboration, mentoring, and the social and professional life of the department. We therefore expect you to be an engaged and visible member of the department's daily academic environment. Your primary responsibilities will include: Conducting and publishing research of high international quality, including in leading journals and publishers within your field. Developing your research profile and contributing to the department's strategic objectives, including through applications for external research funding. Teaching and supervising students at Bachelor's and Master's level and contributing to the continued development of innovative teaching activities. Participating in the supervision of PhD students and contributing to the faculty's PhD courses. Disseminating research findings to academic audiences, stakeholders, and the wider public. Your competencies You have established yourself as a recognised researcher within your field and can document research and teaching qualifications at a high international level. You have a well-developed international network, experience in attracting significant external research funding from national and international funding bodies, and clear potential and motivation to establish yourself as a research leader, to establish your own research group, and to consolidate an independent research agenda. In the assessment process, particular emphasis will be placed on experience gained through employment, visiting appointments, or extended research stays at recognised universities or research institutions outside the candidate's primary institution. Your personal qualities include the ability to build trust-based relationships with colleagues, students, and external partners. As a teacher, you communicate current and relevant knowledge in a clear, engaging, and inspiring manner. As a supervisor, you are professional, inclusive, and supportive. You value collaboration and contribute positively to the collegial culture that characterises the Department of Public Health, the Faculty of Health Sciences, and Aarhus University more broadly. You are motivated to contribute to the department's continued development and to help maintain an inclusive, supportive, and high-performing work environment. Fluency in written and spoken English is required. International applicants are expected to acquire Danish proficiency, and Aarhus University offers language training to support this process. In order to be assessed as qualified for an Associate Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria . Shortlisting will be applied as part of the assessment process. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of Department Michael Baggesen Klitgaard tel.: (+45) 4022 7649. Your place of work will be the Department of Public Health, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Terms of employment Appointment as an associate professor requires research and teaching qualifications at the level that can be achieved through the satisfactory completion of a period of appointment as an assistant professor, but may also be obtained in other ways. The applicant is required to have completed a university pedagogical programme or an equivalent programme. Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . The yearly base salary for a fulltime associate professor is DKK 706.233,06 (including a position related supplement, a pre-agreed associate professor supplememt and pension (18.07%)). Additional supplement(s) can be negotiated dependent on experience, special qualifications and performance of special functions. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Travel and moving expenses may be covered according to the employee in question and, to a limited extent, the accompanying family. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities . Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - associate professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios The five publications stated as the most important in the 'Template for applicant - associate professor' must be submitted Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - associate professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Michael Baggesen Klitgaard Head of Department, Department of Public Health +4540227649 mbk@ph.au.dk Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4520874354 emply.he.hr@au.dk Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 20 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: au@au.dk Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48 English Danish Cookies improve user experience When you click 'Accept all' cookies, Aarhus University can give you the best user experience. Cookies store information about how a user interacts with a website. All your data is anonymised and cannot be used to identify you. You can always change your consent again under ‘Cookies' in the website footer. The university uses its own cookies and cookies set by our partners. Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified Save & Close Accept all Decline all Read more about cookies Hide details List of cookies Cookie Policy Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified These cookies make it possible to use basic website functionality, e.g. navigation etc. The website does not work without these cookies. Cookie report Name Provider / Domain Expires Description be_typo_user TYPO3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is set by our CMS provider; TYPO3 and is used to identify a backend session when a Backend User is logged in to TYPO3 Backend or Frontend. fe_typo_user Typo3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is associated with the Typo3 web content management system. It is generally used as a user session identifier to enable user preferences to be stored, but in many cases it may not actually be needed as it can be set by default by the platform, though this can be prevented by site administrators. In most cases it is set to be destroyed at the end of a browser session. It contains a random identifier rather than any specific user data. ASP.NET_SessionId Microsoft Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session cookie, used by sites written with Miscrosoft .NET based technologies. Usually used to maintain an anonymised user session by the server. JSESSIONID Oracle Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session cookie, used by sites written in JSP. Usually used to maintain an anonymous user session by the server. ARRAffinity Microsoft Corporation .mitstudie.au.dk Session This cookie is set by websites run on the Windows Azure cloud platform. It is used for load balancing to make sure the visitor page requests are routed to the same server in any browsing session. esctx Microsoft Corporation .login.microsoftonline.com Session This cookie is used by Microsoft to securely verify your login information fpc Microsoft Corporation login.microsoftonline.com 4 weeks 2 days This cookie is used by Microsoft to securely verify your login information __cf_bm Cloudflare Inc. .pure.au.dk 29 minutes 59 seconds This cookie is used to distinguish between humans and bots. This is beneficial for the website, in order to make valid reports on the use of their website. __Secure-typo3nonce_ouB7_tePaslDY7zpIHG6wg aias.au.dk Session __cf_bm Cloudflare Inc. .linkedin.com 29 minutes 59 seconds This cookie is used to distingui