    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/associate-professor-assistant-professor-in-statistical-genetics-or-health-data-science-at-the-pioneer-centre-for-smartbiomed": {
      "title": "Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science",
      "description": "Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Public Health - National Centre for Register-based Research - Faculty: Health Sciences Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed Deadline 11 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Public Health - National Centre for Register-based Research Bartholins Allé 2 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 168738 Apply The Department of Public Health at Faculty of Health Sciences at Aarhus University invites applications for a full-time position as Associate or Assistant Professor at the Pioneer Centre for Statistical and computational Methods for Advanced Research to Transform biomedicine (SMARTbiomed) within the field of statistical and machine learning methods development for genetic analysis and causal inference with a focus on developing and applying statistical, machine learning methods, and AI to analyse population-scale health data and omics data. The position has a start date as of 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The appointment as Associate Professor is permanent, whereas appointment as Assistant Professor is a four-year fixed-term position. About the Department of Public Health The Department of Public Health is a research-intensive environment dedicated to advancing both fundamental scientific knowledge and applied solutions to major public health challenges. Our research spans theoretical, basic, and applied sciences, with the shared ambition of generating impactful knowledge and improving population health. Academic excellence is at the core of everything we do. We are committed to conducting rigorous and innovative research and delivering high-quality teaching. The department is responsible for teaching within public health, sport science, health science, nursing science, and related methodological disciplines, as well as a substantial part of the teaching in the medical degree programme. While firmly rooted in our respective disciplines, we actively foster interdisciplinary collaboration to advance innovation and excellence in both research and education. We are equally committed to cultivating a healthy, inclusive, and supportive workplace culture characterized by open dialogue, mutual respect, and an informal atmosphere. We believe that academic excellence and personal well-being are mutually reinforcing and essential to long-term success. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Your job responsibilities As an Associate or Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed, you will contribute to the Centre’s research, teaching, and work environment. You are expected to conduct research at a high international level and to develop an independent academic profile through both individual scholarship and collaboration with colleagues. You are committed to delivering high-quality teaching and supervision and bring collaborative skills, intellectual curiosity, and a willingness to contribute broadly across the educational programmes. You are eager to engage in the academic community at Aarhus University and within SMARTbiomed and to contribute to strengthening the department's national and international profile. We also value academic citizenship. This includes active participation in collegial collaboration, mentoring, and the social and professional life of both the department and the centre. We therefore expect you to be an engaged and visible member of the department's daily academic environment. Your primary responsibilities will include: Conducting and publishing research of high international quality, including in leading journals and publishers within your field. Associate Professors are expected to contribute to and lead one or more major research projects focused on deciphering the causal relationship between psychiatric disorders and risk factors Developing your research profile and contributing to the department's strategic objectives, including through applications for external research funding. Teaching at PhD courses and workshops organized by the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed. Assistant professors are expected to participate in the supervision of PhD students, and Associate Professors are expected to take a leading role and contribute also to the faculty's PhD courses. Disseminating research findings to academic audiences, stakeholders, and the wider public. You will be part of the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed and work closely with researchers at our three hub sites. SMARTbiomed is an international collaboration between Aarhus University, University of Oxford, and University of Copenhagen that aims to advance the development of methods and software for analysis and inference from massive human data, advancing applications in medicine. SMARTbiomed is anchored at the National Centre for Register-based Research (NCRR), Department of Public Health at Aarhus University, and led by Professor Naomi Wray. The position is based at our site in Aarhus, but you can expect some travel to our sites in Oxford and Copenhagen. You can read more about SMARTbiomed here and about NCRR here . For the Associate Professor position, we expect you to be an aspiring group leader. Depending on your level an attractive start package can be negotiated, which may include funding for up to one PhD student and one postdoc. You will report to the Head of the National Centre for Register-based Research, Bjarni Vilhjálmsson. Your competences Applicants for Assistant Professorships You hold a PhD degree in a relevant discipline and have demonstrated the potential to become an internationally recognised researcher within statistical genetics, health data sciences, or related research areas. You can document research and teaching experience and show clear promise in developing an independent research agenda. Experience with international research collaboration and applications for external funding will be considered an advantage. You are expected to demonstrate a clear motivation for establishing yourself as a future research leader and for pursuing career-promoting research grants. Although not a requirement, the assessment process will place emphasis on experience gained through employment or extended research stays at recognised universities or research institutions outside the PhD awarding institution. Applicants for Associate Professorships You have established yourself as a recognised researcher within statistical genetics, health data sciences, or related research areas and can document research and teaching qualifications at a high international level – pre- and postgraduate. You have a well-developed international network, experience in attracting significant external research funding from national and international funding bodies, and clear potential and motivation to establish yourself as a research leader with your own research group and consolidate an independent research agenda. In the assessment process, particular emphasis will be placed on experience gained through employment, visiting appointments, or extended research stays at recognised universities or research institutions outside the candidate's primary institution. For both positions, we expect you to have several of the following qualifications: Background in AI, bioinformatics, statistics, computer science, data science, machine learning, epidemiology, or related fields with a relevant PhD degree (required). Experience with analyzing large genetic data sets, e.g., UK Biobank, All-of-Us, or similar data. Experience with developing statistical and computational methods for analyzing genetic or health data. Experience with supervision of PhD students or postdocs. Experience with writing grant applications for external funding. Knowledge of machine learning and epidemiological methods including causal inference and risk prediction. Your personal qualities include the ability to build trust-based relationships with colleagues, students, and external partners. As a teacher, you communicate current and relevant knowledge in a clear, engaging, and inspiring manner. As a supervisor, you are professional, inclusive, and supportive. You value collaboration and contribute positively to the collegial culture that characterises the Department of Public Health, the Faculty of Health Sciences, SMARTbiomed, and Aarhus University more broadly. You are motivated to contribute to the centre´s and the department´s continued development and to help maintain an inclusive, supportive, and high-performing work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. International applicants are expected to learn Danish, and Aarhus University arranges Danish teaching. In order to be assessed as qualified for an Associate Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria . In order to be assessed as qualified for an Assistant Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria. Shortlisting will be used. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of the National Centre for Register-based Research, Bjarni Vilhjálmsson (email: [email protected] , Phone: +45 24201279) or scientific coordinator at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed, Gitte Bundgaard Christiansen (email: [email protected] , Phone: +45 20826431). Your place of work will be the National Centre for Register-based Research, Department of Public Health, Bartholins Allé 2, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. We expect to conduct interviews end of November – beginning of December 2026. Terms of employment – Assistant professor Appointment as an associate professor requires research and teaching qualifications at the level that can be achieved through the satisfactory completion of a period of appointment as an assistant professor, but may also be obtained in other ways. The applicant is required to have completed a university pedagogical programme or an equivalent programme. The yearly base salary for a fulltime associate professor is DKK 706.233,06 (including a position related supplement, a pre-agreed associate professor supplememt and pension (18.07%)). Additional supplement(s) can be negotiated dependent on experience, special qualifications and performance of special functions. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Travel and moving expenses may be covered according to the employee in question and, to a limited extent, the accompanying family. Terms of employment – Assistant professor Appointment as an assistant professor requires academic qualifications at PhD level. The yearly base salary for a fulltime assistant professor is between DKK 493.2394,35 and DKK 585.322,48 depending on the years of working experience after achieved MSc degree. The base salary includes a position related supplement and pension (18.07 %). Additional supplement(s) for special qualifications can be negotiated. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Other terms of employment Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities . Application – Associate professor Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - associate professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios The five publications stated as the most important in the 'Template for applicant - associate professor' must be submitted Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - associate professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) Application – Assistant professor Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - assistant professor or tenure track assistant professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios A maximum of five of the publications of greatest relevance to the job may be submitted (optional) Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - assistant professor or tenure track assistant professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . Aarhus University also has a Junior Researcher Association and offers career development support. You can read more about these resources here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website . Questions about the position\n                            ? Bjarni Jóhann Vilhjálmsson Professor, Department of Public Health - National Centre for Register-based Research Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4593521835 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday 11 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/scientific-writer-and-communicator-from-cutting-edge-science-to-a-sustainable-biodiverse-future": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Scientific writer and communicator: from cutting-edge science to a sustainable biodiverse future - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Scientific writer and communicator: from cutting-edge science to a sustainable biodiverse future Deadline 12 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 168892 Apply Are you passionate about transforming cutting-edge science into compelling stories that inspire action and understanding? Do you enjoy communicating complex research in ways that engage scientists, decision-makers, and the wider public? Are you an exceptional writer with a talent for storytelling, scientific editing, and creative communication across multiple platforms? Then join us in telling the story of how science can help shape a sustainable future for biodiversity and society. The Department of Biology invites applications for a Scientific Writer and Communications Specialist position. Start date: 1st of January 2027. You will join the Center for Sustainable Landscapes Under Global Change (SustainScapes) and work closely with Professor Signe Normand and the centre’s researchers to bring groundbreaking research to life. The centre investigates how biodiversity and landscapes are changing under global environmental pressures and develops new knowledge to support a more sustainable future. As part of our team, you will help communicate the centre's scientific discoveries, visions, and impacts to diverse audiences. You will play a central role in translating complex scientific results into clear, engaging, and visually appealing stories that showcase the significance of our research and its relevance for society. Your tasks will include: Leading the development of an inspiring coffee table book highlighting the centre's scientific journey, discoveries, people, and impacts. Assisting researchers in editing and refining scientific manuscripts, reports, and policy briefs. Working closely with researchers to identify and communicate emerging discoveries and societal impacts. Creating compelling stories around scientific breakthroughs, field campaigns, technological innovations, and interdisciplinary collaborations. Communicating research findings through interviews, news articles, visual storytelling, and multimedia content. Developing engaging content for the centre's website, newsletters, and social media channels. Expected start date and duration of employment The post position is expected to start 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible hereafter. Job description We are looking for a creative and proactive communicator who combines scientific insight with exceptional writing skills. You should enjoy working in a dynamic research environment and be motivated by helping excellent science reach and inspire a broader audience. This position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to an internationally ambitious research centre at the forefront of biodiversity science, sustainable landscapes, and global change research. Together, we aim not only to advance scientific knowledge but also to communicate that knowledge in ways that inspire engagement, understanding, and positive change. Qualifications You hold a Master's degree in a relevant field, such as biology, ecology, environmental science and/or has several years of experience with scientific writing and communication. A PhD within the mentioned fields is considered an advantage. Specifically, we look for the following qualifications: Outstanding writing and editing skills in English, with the ability to communicate complex scientific concepts clearly and accurately to diverse audiences. Demonstrated experience in scientific writing, communication, journalism, publishing, or related activities. Excellent ability to identify, develop, and communicate engaging narratives from scientific research. Experience editing scientific manuscripts, reports, grant applications, or other research-related documents. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to collaborate closely with researchers from different disciplines. Excellent organizational and project management skills, including the ability to coordinate multiple communication activities and deadlines simultaneously. A proactive, independent, and solution-oriented working style. At the center, we value a collegial and inclusive working environment and place strong emphasis on good relationships between colleagues and students. We therefore seek candidates with an open, constructive, and collaborative approach The Department of Biology The Department of Biology ( http://bio.au.dk/ ) provides a framework for research and teaching in all major biological subdisciplines. The department is especially known for its research contribution in biodiversity, global change and conservation biology, aquatic biology and ecology, arctic environment and ecosystems, genetics and evolution, microbiology and zoophysiology. The department currently encompasses approximately 200 academic and technical staff and 45 PhD students while 400 students follow the BSc and MSc programs offered by the institute. The Department offers a vibrant and informal research environment with a long-standing tradition of collaboration with international university partners. Much of the internal communication is in English and we welcome international applicants. What we offer an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a work environment encouraging lively, open and critical discussion a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of employment is Aarhus University, Department of Biology, Section for Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity (ECOINF), Ny Munkegade 114, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. The work involves some periods of field work away from Aarhus. Contact information For further information, please contact: Prof. Signe Normand, +45 93 52 28 77, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 12 October 2026 Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Signe Normand Professor, Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity +4593522877 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522521469 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 12 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-position-diversifying-embodiment-in-education-overcoming-inequity-in-embodied-learning-in-danish-vocational-education-and-beyond": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position: Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Emdrup - Faculty: Arts Postdoctoral position: Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond Deadline 13 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Emdrup Tuborgvej 164 2400 København NV Full-time position ID: 168244 Apply The Danish School of Education (DPU) at the Faculty of Arts, Aarhus University, invites applications for a postdoctoral position within the research project Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond , funded by Independent Research Fund Denmark. This is a full-time (37 hours per week), fixed-term (24 months) postdoctoral position, starting on 1 January 2027 and ending on 31 December 2028. The position is based within the Department of Education Studies at DPU’s campus in Emdrup, although the successful applicant should expect some travel activity between the department’s sites in Aarhus and Copenhagen. The Danish School of Education is committed to diversity and encourages all qualified applicants to apply, regardless of their personal background. Given the project’s empirical focus on educational settings in Denmark, applicants must be sufficiently proficient in the Danish language to conduct fieldwork and actively participate in these settings. Research Context The postdoctoral research will form part of the broader research project Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond . The project addresses a key issue in contemporary education: how educational practices cultivate, recognise, and value certain bodies and sensory experiences in learning contexts while marginalising others, and how these processes contribute to educational inequities. Combining perspectives from educational studies, anthropology, embodiment studies, and critical disability studies, the project examines how different bodies and sensoria (i.e., culturally shaped ways of sensing and engaging with the world) are formed and valued through the corporeal, social, and material interactions that shape embodied learning. Danish vocational education and training (VET) serves as the empirical case. Within VET, students move between classrooms, workshops, and workplaces as apprentices, developing skills through interactions with teachers, peers, colleagues, technologies, tools, and materials. The project’s methodology draws on ethnographic and sensory approaches to examine the corporeal, social, and material dynamics that shape embodied learning across four vocational programmes (to be selected in collaboration with the postdoctoral researcher). The project comprises three subprojects. The postdoctoral researcher will collaborate with the Principal Investigator on one of these subprojects, examining interactions among students, teachers, and nonhuman actors within the four selected vocational programmes. In addition, the postdoctoral researcher will lead a second subproject focused on interactions among apprentices, workplace instructors, and nonhuman actors in workplace settings. Based on approximately three months of ethnographic fieldwork in workplaces associated with the four selected vocational programmes, this subproject will explore how instructors introduce new apprentices to embodied practices and perceptions of their future profession, and how they evaluate apprentices’ attempts to engage with these practices and perceptions. Particular attention will be paid to the role of nonhuman actors, such as materials, tools, assistive devices, and instructions, and to how apprentices and instructors interact with and through them. The position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to emerging international debates on embodiment, diversity, and educational inequality, and to advancing innovative theoretical and methodological approaches to the study of embodied learning. Further information Applicants are encouraged to contact project manager Maia Ebsen ( [email protected] ) for further information about the project. Postdoctoral position The selected applicant will work in close collaboration with the project manager, Maia Ebsen, throughout the period of employment. This will include planning and conducting fieldwork, processing and analysing empirical material, and organising seminars with the project’s advisory board. In addition to conducting and completing two subprojects, one in collaboration with the PI and one independently, the postdoctoral researcher is expected to: Actively engage in collaboration as a key part of the research project Publish one single-author peer-reviewed article) and one peer-reviewed article co-authored with the PI in relevant international journals Initiate, co-organise and participate in dissemination and knowledge exchange activities with the PI (e.g. conferences, seminars and other relevant academic events) Undertake a research visit of minimum two months at a relevant research institution outside Denmark Help disseminate research findings to wider audiences Contribute more broadly to the academic environment at the Department of Education Studies, establishing and maintaining good working relationships with both colleagues and students Teaching and supervision As a postdoctoral researcher, your position is primarily research-based but will also involve a limited amount of teaching and supervision. To that end, the selected applicant will be expected to participate in teaching and supervision activities and teach and supervise at BA and MA levels at the Department of Education Studies. Any teaching activities will be agreed with the head of department and/or the project PI. Qualifications Applicants should be able to document: solid experience in planning, conducting, analysing and disseminating ethnographic fieldwork an internationally oriented research profile, as documented by a PhD dissertation and/or research publications experience in independently managing research projects relevant teaching experience at university level commensurate with the length of the applicant’s academic career good communication and interpersonal skills for engaging productively with external organisations and institutions Furthermore, applicants who can document the following will have an advantage: a basic understanding of the Danish VET system a track record of research publications of a high international standard commensurate with the length of the applicant’s academic career experience of participating in national and international research networks time spent working at internationally recognised research institutions outside Denmark Applicants must also speak and write English at an advanced academic level. The application must be submitted in English. It must include a cover letter, a CV (including a list of publications), publications if relevant to the position (maximum two), and a teaching statement (or equivalent documentation of teaching experience and qualifications). Moreover, applicants are asked to include a research statement (approximately two pages). This should include a description of how their previous research experience and qualifications are relevant to the position, and a proposal for research to be undertaken within the framework for the research project outlined above, outlining a methodological and conceptual approach for the subproject led by the postdoc. A timetable should be included for completing this subproject within the period of employment. Lastly, the application must include a copy of the applicant’s PhD degree certificate or other documentation confirming the expected date of degree completion. Please note that applications lacking such documentation will not be considered. Although the Aarhus University application system includes an option to upload letters of recommendation, applicants should not include letters of recommendation or references with the application. Applicants who are invited to an interview may be asked to provide references. For further information about the position, please contact Miriam Madsen , head of the Department of Education Studies, email: [email protected] , tel.: +4520842672 Read about the Department of Education Studies here . About the Danish School of Education The Danish School of Education at Aarhus University is Denmark’s largest centre for research and teaching in the fields of education, didactics and learning, with approximately 240 full-time researchers, including 80 PhD students, and 4,500 Bachelor’s and Master’s degree students. The school’s activities are characterised by a high degree of interdisciplinarity and close interaction with society, including businesses, organisations, government agencies and institutions, both in Denmark and abroad. International applicants International applicants are encouraged to read about family and work-life balance as well as the attractive working conditions and other benefits of working at Aarhus University and in Denmark, including healthcare, paid holidays and, if relevant, maternity/paternity leave, childcare and schooling. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including a relocation service and career counselling for expat partners. For information about taxation, see the taxation aspects of international researchers’ employment by AU . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here: http://international.au.dk/research/ Aarhus University also offers a junior researcher development programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here: https://talent.au.dk/junior-researcher-development-programme/ Questions about the position\n                            ? Miriam Madsen Associate Professor, Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Aarhus +4520842672 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - HR, AR, Emdrup +4593508310 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 13 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/assistant-professors-in-analytic-number-theory-automorphic-forms-and-representation-theory": {
      "title": "Assistant Professors in Analytic Number Theory, Automorphic Forms and Representation Theory",
      "description": "Assistant Professors in Analytic Number Theory, Automorphic Forms and Representation Theory - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Mathematics Assistant Professors in Analytic Number Theory, Automorphic Forms and Representation Theory Deadline 26 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Feb Department of Mathematics Ny Munkegade 118 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Feb 2027 - 31 Jan 2029 ID: 168287 Apply The Department of Mathematics at Aarhus University invites applications for two two-year Assistant Professor positions in Analytic Number Theory, Automorphic Forms and Representation Theory , available from February 1, 2027. The successful candidates will be affiliated with the \"Estimates for L-functions\" project, led by Professor Paul D. Nelson and funded by the Villum Foundation . The project will concern research in some part of analytic number theory, automorphic forms and/or representation theory, building on recent advances in these subjects. Qualifications Applicants should hold a PhD degree in mathematics and have a strong research record in analytic number theory, automorphic forms, representation theory, or related areas. The ability to conduct independent research, as well as to collaborate effectively within the research group, will be emphasized. Experience in teaching at the university level and the ability to contribute to the department’s research-based teaching activities (including associated examination duties) in mathematics are expected. The position may also include knowledge exchange with society. We offer A stimulating research environment with a strong international profile. Opportunities to collaborate with leading experts in analytic number theory and related fields. A well-supported research infrastructure, including access to travel funds and research visitors. A vibrant department with active seminar series and workshops. About the department The Department of Mathematics at Aarhus University is internationally recognized for excellence in research and teaching. It hosts several research groups in pure and applied mathematics and offers a dynamic and collegial academic atmosphere. More information about the department can be found at https://math.au.dk . Place of work and area of employment The place of work is the Department of Mathematics, Aarhus University, Ny Munkegade 118, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Contact information For further information, please contact Professor Paul David Nelson, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 26 October 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities. Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Paul David Nelson Professor, Department of Mathematics +4587151781 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520612514 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 26 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-researcher-in-environmental-genomics-and-pollinator-health": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Environmental Genomics and Pollinator Health",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Environmental Genomics and Pollinator Health - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Ecoscience - Centre for Nature-based Solutions - Faculty: Technical Sciences Postdoctoral Researcher in Environmental Genomics and Pollinator Health Deadline 30 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Feb Department of Ecoscience - Centre for Nature-based Solutions Frederiksborgvej 399 4000 Roskilde Fixed term full-time position 1 Feb 2027 - 31 Jan 2029 ID: 168179 Apply Join a growing interdisciplinary research team developing next-generation tools for biodiversity monitoring and environmental genomics. The Department of Ecoscience at Aarhus University invites applications for a Villum Foundation funded postdoctoral position in environmental genomics, microbiome ecology and pollinator health. Pollinator declines represent one of the most pressing challenges facing biodiversity conservation worldwide. This project investigates how freshwater habitats influence pollinator microbiomes, physiology and ecological interactions. By combining environmental genomics, microbiome science, field ecology and biodiversity monitoring, the project aims to uncover novel links between aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems. The position is located in Risø and is available from 1 February 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter, for a fixed-term period of 2 years. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a two-year position from 1 February 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description Pollinator declines represent one of the most pressing challenges facing biodiversity conservation worldwide. While research has traditionally focused on terrestrial habitats and floral resources, freshwater ecosystems may provide critical but overlooked resources that support pollinator diversity, resilience and health. This project investigates how freshwater habitats influence pollinator microbiomes, physiology and ecological interactions. By combining environmental genomics, microbiome science, field ecology and biodiversity monitoring, the project aims to uncover novel links between aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems. The selected candidate will lead research investigating microbial communities associated with freshwater habitats and pollinator species, with a particular focus on identifying ecological and functional connections between environmental and host-associated microbiomes. The postdoctoral researcher will work closely with a PhD student and an international network of collaborators in ecology, envi-ronmental genomics, pollinator biology and biodiversity monitoring. Key Responsibilities The post doc will: Design and coordinate large-scale field studies across multiple seasons. Collect and process environmental and insect samples for DNA sequencing. Analyse microbial communities associated with freshwater habi-tats and pollinators. Direct and support to the analysis of high-throughput sequencing datasets, including eDNA, metabarcoding and metagenomic, data. Integrate molecular, ecological and environmental datasets to address research questions related to biodiversity, pollinator health and nature restoration. Co-supervising MSc and PhD students. Publishing research in leading international journals. Participating in international collaborations and scientific conferences. Your profile Applicants should hold a PhD in ecology, molecular ecology, genomics, microbiology, bioinformatics, environmental science, or a related discipline. We seek candidates with experience in several of the following areas: Pollinator or insect ecology. Microbial ecology. Environmental DNA (eDNA). Environmental genomics. Metagenomics and/or metabarcoding. Bioinformatics and analysis of high-throughput sequencing data. Statistical analysis using R and/or Python. Reproducible computational workflows. Scientific writing and publication. Microbiome research and host-associated microbial communities. The ideal candidate will have a solid publication record relative to career stage and demonstrate the ability to work both independently and within interdisciplinary teams. No candidate is expected to meet all criteria. Applicants with complementary backgrounds and a strong interest in expanding their expertise in insect ecology, microbiome analyses, shotgun meta-genomics, microbial functional annotation, and high-performance computing are encouraged to apply. Who we are The Department of Ecoscience at Aarhus University is engaged in research programs and advisory work covering the major biological sub-disciplines. We conduct innovative, advanced research in the areas of aquatic biology and ecology, Arctic environments and ecosystems, biodiversity, conservation biology, and wildlife management. The Department currently employs approximately 300 academic and technical staff, as well as many PhD students. The qualified candidate will work in a supportive and internationally engaged scientific environment at the Centre for Nature-based Solutions . The department aims to be a supportive and encourag-ing workplace that offers interesting challenges, collaborative colleagues, and avenues for academic growth. The department actively facilitates a healthy integration of work and personal life and aspires to recruit and maintain highly skilled individuals who prioritise curiosity and trust. What we offer The department/centre offers: a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Aarhus University, Frederiksborgvej 399, DK-4000 Roskilde, Denmark, approximately 40 km west of Copenhagen. Contact information For further information, please contact: Georgina Brennna ( [email protected] ) Deadline Applications must be received no later than 30 October 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Georgina Lauren Brennan Tenure Track Researcher, Department of Ecoscience - Centre for Nature-based Solutions [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Friday 30 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-position-in-the-speedyfermhub-research-project": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in the SpeedyFermHub research project",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position in the SpeedyFermHub research project - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Management - Aarhus BSS Postdoctoral position in the SpeedyFermHub research project Deadline 1 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Mar Department of Management Universitetsbyen 61 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2029 ID: 168236 Apply Will you be part of shaping the future of fermented plant-based foods? Do you want to explore how consumers respond to novel plant-based food innovations and how consumer insights can inform product development, market positioning and societal value? Then join the SpeedyFermHub research project as a postdoctoral researcher at the Department of Management, Aarhus University. We invite applications for a 24-month postdoctoral position starting in 2027. The position offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a new research project focusing on consumer-citizen acceptance, market positioning and the societal relevance of fermented plant-based food innovations. The starting date is 1 March 2027 or subject to mutual agreement. The research project The postdoctoral position is part of the externally funded FutureFoodS project SpeedyFermHub – which aims to support the development, optimization and scale-up of fermented plant-based food prototypes. Within the project, the Department of Management at Aarhus University contributes expertise in consumer behavior and food marketing, with a particular focus on consumer-citizen acceptance, stakeholder-informed product development, market relevance and societal positioning. The project is expected to generate high-quality research on how consumers evaluate novel fermented plant-based foods and how consumer insights can inform product refinement, communication strategies and market positioning. Job description The successful candidate is expected to contribute to the overall objectives of the project by being involved in design, implementation, data collection, and analysis of consumer-citizen studies related to fermented plant-based food prototypes.  Therefore, the candidate will contribute to the development of online experimental consumer and sensory studies, including the design of product concepts, survey materials, experimental stimuli and measures of consumer preferences, acceptance and willingness-to-pay. The candidate is also expected to contribute to the other phases of the research process, including in-person consumer testing of final prototypes, and to support the assessment of market trends, entry potential and societal positioning of fermented plant-based products. The candidate will be involved in data collection, quantitative data analysis, interpretation of findings, preparation of deliverables, writing of academic articles and ongoing development of the project in collaboration with national and international partners. The position is research-oriented but may involve teaching assignments. Qualifications Candidates must hold a PhD relevant to the academic areas of the Department of Management, (preferably within one or more of the following areas: consumer behavior, food marketing, marketing, sustainable consumption, behavioral science, agricultural economics, economics, psychology, or related fields.). The ideal candidate will have experience with one or more of the following: consumer research on food, sustainability, health, innovation or novel foods; survey design and online experimental methods; quantitative data analysis, preferably including choice modelling, willingness-to-pay analysis, segmentation, multivariate statistics, or related methods; consumer acceptance, product concept testing, or sensory-consumer research; academic writing and publication in peer-reviewed journals; collaboration in interdisciplinary and international research environments. Experience with software such as R, Python, SPSS, Stata, Sawtooth, Qualtrics or similar tools will be considered an advantage. The successful candidate should have strong analytical skills, good communication skills, the ability to work independently, and the ability to collaborate with researchers and partners from different disciplinary backgrounds. Excellent written and spoken English is required. Who we are The Department of Management is part of Aarhus BSS, Aarhus University – a top 100 university. Aarhus BSS has achieved the triple-crown AACSB, AMBA and EQUIS accreditations. The department has an international research and teaching environment, employs approximately 160 Danish and international researchers, academic staff, and PhD students. We excel in business management, including strategy, innovation, marketing, organisation, digitalisation, and accounting. Our aim is to deliver high-quality research, with a strong emphasis on publishing in leading academic journals and presenting at recognised conferences. You can read more about the Department of Management at: http://mgmt.au.dk. Further information For further information about the position and the department, please contact Head of Department/project facilitator Associate Professor, Marija Banovic, Tel.: +45 52609827, Email: [email protected] If you need help uploading your application or have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR Coordinator Maria Grauert Løvgaard, Email: [email protected] International applicant? Find information about entering and working in Denmark here Place of work Aarhus BSS Department of Management Universitetsbyen 61 International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information here: https://internationalstaff.au.dk/relocationservice/ Please find more information about research opportunities at Aarhus University here: http://international.au.dk/research/ Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here: http://talent.au.dk/junior-researcher-development-programme/ Terms of employment Salary and terms of employment are governed by the Cirkulære om overenskomst for akademikere i staten (in Danish). You will be employed as Post doc at base pay level 4–8 with additional supplements in accordance with the Cirkulære om protokollat om visse ansættelsesvilkår for videnskabeligt personale ved universiteter (in Danish). The job content and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of  Academic Staff at Universities . Application procedure When you apply for this position it is mandatory to attach the following: Application Curriculum Vitae. You are encouraged to declare any periods of leave without research activity, including, for example, maternity leave, since your research activities are assessed in relation to your actual research time Education (diploma for master's, PhD and possibly higher doctoral degree) List of publications (the enclosed publications must be clearly marked on the list of publications) Publications. Up to three publications can be included in the application. In the event of several authors the publications must be accompanied by a co-author statement concerning the applicant's share of the collaborative work with the consent of the co-authors. This template may be used for the purpose Teaching portfolio. The specific requirements regarding the documentation can be found here Materials which cannot be uploaded together with the application may be submitted in three copies to Aarhus BSS HR & PhD, Aarhus University, Bartholins Allé 16, DK-8000 Aarhus C. Read more about how to apply for an academic post at Aarhus BSS here The evaluation process After the application deadline, an assessment committee is appointed. Please note that the assessment of applicants is based solely on the material received prior to the application deadline. For further information, see Guidelines for Assessment Committee, Aarhus BSS . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Aarhus University Aarhus University is an academically diverse and research-intensive university with a strong commitment to high-quality research and education and the development of society nationally and globally. The university offers an inspiring research and teaching environment to its 37,000 students (FTEs) and 8,700 employees and has an annual revenue of EUR 1.106 billion. Learn more at www.international.au.dk/ Questions about the position\n                            ? Marija Banovic Associate Professor, Department of Management +4587165215 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Aarhus BSS Administrative Centre - Aarhus BSS HR and PhD Administration +4587153445 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday  1 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-in-aerobiological-modelling": {
      "title": "PostDoc in aerobiological modelling",
      "description": "PostDoc in aerobiological modelling - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Environmental Science - Faculty: Technical Sciences PostDoc in aerobiological modelling Deadline 1 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Mar Department of Environmental Science Frederiksborgvej 399 4000 Roskilde Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2029 ID: 167940 Apply PostDoc in aerobiological modelling Are you interested in atmospheric science and can you contribute to the development of a new generation of numerical models describing concentrations and transport of bioaerosols? T hen the Department of Environmental Science invites you to apply for a 2 year post doc position, with possibility for extension. The position is associated with two research projects funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation: 1) NNF RECRUIT, 2022-2029 and NNF EARLY-BLIGHT, 2026-2029. The position is associated with two research projects funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation: 1) NNF RECRUIT, 2022-2029 and NNF EARLY-BLIGHT, 2026-2029. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a 2 year position from 1 March 2027 or as soon possible. This is a fixed-term position to end on 28 February 2029, but with possibility for an extension. Job description •    You will be contributing to further development of the WRF-Chem model to handle a large range of bioaerosols in particular pollen and fungal spores. The geographical focus is on both the urban environment and agricultural areas in Denmark •    You will be contributing to the dynamical integration of near real-time data into WRF-Chem •    You will be contributing to the development of machine learning models used on data from Poleno Jupiters, applying Python and machine learning. •    The position will focus on implementing grass pollen (both generic grasses and specific grass species) and selected fungal spores such as Alternaria alternata and Phytophtora infestans •    You will work with data obtained with Poleno Jupiter instruments, with data obtained with DNA methods and data obtained with a Hirst trap. •    Numerical models used by the group covers the particle dispersion model HYSPLIT in combination and the numerical models WRF and WRF-Chem •    You will be working in an aerobiological research group funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation containing two additional post docs, a PhD student and a professor. This teams covers expertise on numerical modelling, atmosphere, microbiology and bioinformatics •    These activities will be in close collaboration with a group of technicians •    Disseminate results is a key aspect, which involves the production of manuscripts, both as co-author and lead author, for international peer-reviewed publications and participation in relevant conferences. Your profile Applicants should hold a PhD in environmental science, physics, chemistry, mathematics, engineering or similar. You need to demonstrate experience with collaboration and presenting works at international conferences and leading manuscripts published in international peer reviewed journals. Understanding of the atmosphere and its modelling is essential. •    Model development of WRF-Chem is complex. Documented experience using FORTRAN, C or similar is therefore required •    A substantial fraction of the technical part will be handled on high performance computers and the AI supercomputer GEFION, all operated in a Linux Environment. Documented experience with Linux is therefore also required •    Candidates with a completed PhD degree before the interview date will also be considered •    Documented research experience on numerical modelling •    Experience with either WRF, WRF-Chem or bioaerosols is a considerable advantage but not needed for the right candidate •    Experience with PyTorch and Tensorflow in Python is an advantage, but not needed for the right candidate Further requirements are English fluency, good writing and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and in an international academic environment. Department of Environmental Science The Department of Environmental Science is an interdisciplinary department under the Faculty of Technical Sciences at Aarhus University. The expertise of the department ranges from physics, chemistry, microbiology, social science, geography, economics, to policy analysis. Basic as well as applied research is conducted on some of the major challenges facing society, such as pollution and pollution control mechanisms, management of land, soil, water, air and biodiversity, protection of ecosystem services, and climate change. Advisory services within these areas are offered to ministries and other authorities. Currently, the department employs about 140 staff, postdocs and PhD students. We believe in values of equality, diversity and inclusion, and creating an attractive work/life balance. Further information can be found at www.envs.au.dk . The selected candidate will be affiliated with the Section for Atmospheric Environment in the Group of Atmospheric Measurements. Due to the interdisciplinary work, be affiliated with the interdisciplinary iCLIMATE Aarhus University Interdisciplinary Centre for Climate Change ( https://iclimate.au.dk/ ) What we offer The department offers: •    a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment •    an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators •    a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research •    a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities •    a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. The project offers •    A well funded research group •    Access to one of the longest pollen records in Europe for model development, collected with a Hirst trap •    Access to data from 3 Poleno Jupiters for model development. These Instrument that have been running in Denmark since 2025 •    Access to DNA based observations for model development, both quantitative data and sequencing data, covering Denmark and selected European countries Place of work and area of employment The place of work will be at Aarhus University, Frederiksborgvej 399, 4000 Roskilde, Denmark. The city of Roskilde ( www.roskilde.dk ) is located approx. 30 km from Copenhagen. The area provides immediate access to a wealth of cultural and recreational pursuits. International candidates may find information concerning living and working in Denmark at www.workindenmark.dk . AU International Center offers a full range of services to make your transition to Denmark as smooth as possible ( http://www.au.dk/en/internationalcentre/ias /), and the department will assist foreign candidates with finding a suitable place to live. Contact information Further information may be obtained by contacting Professor in Aerobiology Carsten Ambelas Skjøth ( [email protected] ), https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/persons/ [email protected] or head of section Lise Lotte Sørensen ( [email protected] ), https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/persons/ [email protected] Deadline Applications must be received no later than 1 November 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Carsten Ambelas Skjøth Professor, Department of Environmental Science - Atmospheric Measurements +4593522737 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520735558 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday  1 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/post-doc-data-integration-for-biodiversity-dynamics": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Post doc: Data integration for biodiversity dynamics - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Post doc: Data integration for biodiversity dynamics Deadline 11 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 168900 Apply Do you enjoy finding solutions to integrate and analyse large data sets of biodiversity dynamics and their drivers? Are you creative and able to couple various data flows and integrated modelling and analyses based on various input data sets from eDNA, remote sensing and biodiversity observations? Department of Biology advertises a postdoc position focused on data integration and visualisation. The position is primarily funded by the Center for Sustainable Landscapes Under Global Change (SustainScapes) and you will be working directly with Professor Signe Normand, but where relevant there is also the option to collaborate with Fabian Schneider and other researchers at the Center for Landscape Research in Sustainable Agricultural Futures (Land-CRAFT). Expected start date and duration of employment This is a two-year post doc position expected to start 1 January 2027, or as soon as possible hereafter. Job description We are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to explore one of the most pressing scientific challenges of our time: understanding how biodiversity is changing in response to multiple, interacting global change drivers. The project will combine cutting-edge analyses of large biodiversity datasets with the integration of diverse geospatial and remote sensing data sources to quantify environmental stressors and their impacts on ecosystems. This position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to high-impact research at the forefront of biodiversity science and global change ecology. You will work on combining extensive biodiversity datasets, innovative geospatial products, and remotely sensed observations to generate new insights into the causes and consequences of biodiversity change in Denmark and Europe. The position provides an exciting opportunity to contribute to internationally leading research, collaborate with an interdisciplinary team, and develop novel approaches for understanding and predicting biodiversity futures in a rapidly changing world. Qualifications Ideally, you hold a PhD in biology, earth system or data science, or a similar field, and have several years of experience with interdisciplinary collaborations focused on understanding biodiversity dynamics by integrating ecological and environmental data and knowledge across scales. Specifically, we look for the following qualifications: Proven expertise in modelling and analyses of biodiversity dynamics across large spatial and temporal scales. Experience in the conceptualisation and development of new methodological approaches for analysing and modelling species range dynamics, functional trait variation, or species interactions. Proven skills in integration and utilizing various remotely sensed products across scales, including both spectral and structural data streams. Strong documented skills in advanced statistics, AI and machine learning methods, including demonstrated experience in analysing multiple global change drivers, e.g. land use intensity, climate change, nitrogen deposition. Proven capability to acquire, download, manage, and synthesise large geospatial datasets from diverse sources. Proven ability to publish at a high international level. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English are required. Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills, with a demonstrated ability to work effectively across disciplines and employee groups. At the centres, we value a collegial and inclusive working environment and place strong emphasis on good relationships between colleagues and students. We therefore seek candidates with an open, constructive, and collaborative approach. Who we are The Department of Biology (http://bio.au.dk/) provides a framework for research and teaching in all major biological subdisciplines. The department is especially known for its research contribution in biodiversity, global change and conservation biology, aquatic biology and ecology, arctic environment and ecosystems, genetics and evolution, microbiology and zoophysiology. The department currently encompasses approximately 200 academic and technical staff and 45 PhD students while 400 students follow the BSc and MSc programs offered by the institute. The department offers a vibrant and informal research environment with a long-standing tradition of collaboration with international university partners. Much of the internal communication is in English and we welcome international applicants. What we offer an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a work environment encouraging lively, open and critical discussion a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of employment is Aarhus University, Department of Biology, Section for Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity (ECOINF), Ny Munkegade 114, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. The work involves some periods of field work away from Aarhus. Contact information For further information, please contact: Prof. Signe Normand, +45 93 52 28 77, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 11 October 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Signe Normand Professor, Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity +4593522877 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday 11 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/institut-for-virksomhedsledelse-soeger-en-erfaren-og-udadvendt-ledelseskoordinator-og-pa-til-institutleder": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Institut for Virksomhedsledelse søger en erfaren og udadvendt ledelseskoordinator og PA til institutleder - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Management Institut for Virksomhedsledelse søger en erfaren og udadvendt ledelseskoordinator og PA til institutleder Deadline 15 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Dec Department of Management Universitetsbyen 61 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 169057 Apply Har du erfaring med ledelsesbetjening og administrative processer? Trives du med at koordinere opgaver på tværs af en organisation, skabe overblik og understøtte ledelsen i en travl hverdag? Så er du måske vores nye ledelseskoordinator og PA til institutleder ved Institut for Virksomhedsledelse. Som ledelseskoordinator og PA til institutleder får du en central rolle på instituttet, hvor du både understøtter institutledelsen og koordinerer HR-relaterede processer og opgaver i tæt samarbejde med HR. Du bliver en vigtig samarbejdspartner for institutlederen og ledelsesgruppen og får samtidig en bred kontaktflade til medarbejdere, administrative enheder og samarbejdspartnere på Aarhus Universitet. Stillingen kræver, at du kan bevare overblikket, når der er mange opgaver og processer i gang på samme tid, og at du trives med både de planlagte opgaver og løbende behov, der opstår i en organisation med mange forskellige interessenter. Om stillingen Dine primære arbejdsopgaver vil være: PA for institutlederen og understøtte institutlederen i den daglige ledelsesbetjening, ledelsessparring og forskellige ad hoc-opgaver. Forberede og koordinere ledelsesmøder, herunder struktur, materialehåndtering og opfølgning. Følge op på ledelsesbeslutninger, sikre fremdrift samt koordinere sager og processer på tværs af administrative enheder og fagmiljøer. Varetage administrative opgaver og -processer, herunder personaleadministration samt on- og offboarding i samarbejde HR og andre administrative kolleger. Koordinere og facilitere diverse institutarrangementer. Varetage udvalgsbetjening af instituttets Institutforum og Diversitets- og Ligestillingsudvalg. Være bindeled og kontaktperson mellem institutlederen, medarbejdere, samarbejdspartnere og eksterne aktører, herunder særligt HR. Du vil spille en central rolle i at understøtte instituttets ledelse og sikre, at administrative opgaver og processer bliver koordineret og fulgt til dørs. Du vil referere til institutlederen. Om dig Vi leder efter en erfaren, struktureret og selvstændig kollega, som trives i en udadvendt rolle med mange forskellige samarbejdsrelationer. Du har et godt overblik, tager ansvar og følger dine opgaver til dørs. Samtidig er du god til at skabe relationer og samarbejde med mennesker på tværs af organisationen. Du kan bevare roen og overblikket i travle perioder, hvor der er mange bolde i luften, og du har en naturlig evne til at prioritere og skabe struktur. Du er initiativrig, har personligt drive og trives i en dynamisk hverdag, hvor opgaverne kan ændre sig undervejs. Du kommunikerer ubesværet på både dansk og engelsk i skrift og tale. Instituttet har et internationalt forskningsmiljø, og en stor del af vores hverdag foregår på engelsk. Vi forestiller os, at du: har en relevant akademisk uddannelse på kandidatniveau kombineret med flere års administrativ erfaring - eventuelt fra Aarhus Universitet eller lignende institution. har erfaring med ledelsesbetjening og -understøttelse. har solid erfaring med administrative opgaver og gerne kendskab til HR-relaterede processer. har erfaring med administrativ understøttelse af mødefora. har personligt drive, er initiativrig og god til at samarbejde. er en stærk kommunikator og kan håndtere mange interessenter og komplekse problemstillinger med en tolerant og empatisk tilgang. kan arbejde selvstændigt og målrettet og er god til at overholde deadlines. er fortrolig med administrative IT-værktøjer og -systemer og har let ved at sætte dig ind i nye systemer. Vi tilbyder En central rolle på instituttet, hvor du får mulighed for at præge din arbejdsdag og være en vigtig støtte for ledelsen. Et positivt og udviklende arbejdsmiljø med fokus på samarbejde, respekt og faglighed. Mulighed for faglig og personlig udvikling i en dynamisk organisation. Hvem er vi? Institut for Virksomhedsledelse er et af de seks institutter ved Aarhus BSS – et af Aarhus Universitets fem fakulteter. Aarhus BSS samler de kernefaglige erhvervs- og samfundsvidenskabelige forskningsområder og bringer dem tættere sammen for at afspejle det tætte forhold mellem samfund og erhvervsliv. Aarhus Universitet er på listen over verdens 100 bedste universiteter, og Aarhus BSS og Institut for Virksomhedsledelse har opnået de prestigefyldte AACSB-, AMBA- og EQUIS-akkrediteringer. Instituttet beskæftiger mere end 160 akademiske medarbejdere, herunder forskere, ph.d.-studerende og tilknyttede forskere. Du kan læse mere om Institut for Virksomhedsledelse på http://mgmt.au.dk/ . Du bliver en del af instituttets sekretariat med 20 medarbejdere, hvor vi løser administrative opgaver inden for undervisning og forskning samt øvrige administrative opgaver på instituttet. I sekretariatet vægter vi et godt og trygt arbejdsmiljø højt, og kollegial støtte og humor er en naturlig del af hverdagen. Vi arbejder tæt sammen og hjælper hinanden på tværs af opgaver og udfordringer. Vores fælles mål er ikke kun at levere høj kvalitet i arbejdet, men også at sikre en arbejdsplads, hvor alle trives og føler sig godt tilpas. Du kan læse mere om sekretariatet her: https://mgmt.au.dk/contact/ Yderligere information Yderligere information om stillingen og instituttet kan fås ved henvendelse til sekretariatsleder Lone Larsen, tlf. +45 93 50 83 38, e-mail: [email protected] . Hvis du har brug for hjælp til at uploade din ansøgning eller har spørgsmål til rekrutteringsprocessen, er du velkommen til at kontakte HR-koordinator Maria Grauert Løvgaard, tlf. +45 8715 3445, e-mail: [email protected] . Stillingen er en fast fuldtidsstilling med forventet startdato 1. december 2026 eller snarest muligt derefter. Ansøgningsproces Alle ansøgninger skal være modtaget online via Statens Rekrutteringsløsning senest den 15. oktober 2026. Første samtalerunde forventes afholdt i uge 43 og eventuel anden samtalerunde kort derefter. Krav og vilkår Løn- og ansættelsesvilkår følger overenskomsten for akademikere i staten. Du ansættes som fuldmægtig i basisløntrin 4-8. Aarhus Universitet Aarhus Universitet vil være en attraktiv og inspirerende arbejdsplads for alle og ønsker en kultur, hvor hver enkelt kan udfolde og udvikle sig. Vi ser ligestilling og diversitet som en ressource og opfordrer derfor alle interesserede til at ansøge. Ansøgning sendes via Aarhus Universitets rekrutteringssystem, som kan tilgås under stillingsopslaget på Aarhus Universitets hjemmeside. Questions about the position\n                            ? Lone Larsen Head of Secretariat, Department of Management +4593508338 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Aarhus BSS Administrative Centre - Aarhus BSS HR and PhD Administration +4587153445 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday 15 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/electronics-tenure-track-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Electronics – Tenure Track Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Electronics – Tenure Track Assistant Professor - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Biomedical Engineering - Faculty: Technical Sciences Electronics – Tenure Track Assistant Professor Deadline 15 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Dec Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Biomedical Engineering Finlandsgade 22 8200 Aarhus N Full-time position ID: 169021 Apply The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at Aarhus University (AU) invites applications for a tenure-track position as Assistant Professor in Electronics. We seek a talented and ambitious researcher who can contribute to strengthening and further developing our research and education activities in electronics. The position is broadly defined within electronics, with particular interest in candidates working with sensors, instrumentation, and electronic measurement systems, as well as electronic technologies for healthcare and medical applications, industrial systems, energy conversion, and energy-efficient electronics. The position offers an opportunity to establish an independent research profile in a flourishing interdisciplinary environment with strong links across engineering disciplines and to industrial and healthcare partners. Expected start date and duration of employment 01.12.2026 Job description The successful candidate is expected to develop an independent and internationally recognized research profile within electronics. The candidate will be encouraged and supported in establishing a research activity based on competitive external funding and in developing collaborations within and outside the department. Research is expected to combine strong fundamentals in electronics with experimental development, implementation, and validation of novel electronic technologies and systems. We particularly value research that bridges electronic components and circuits with complete measurement and instrumentation systems. The candidate will contribute to teaching within the department's Bachelor's and Master's degree programs, including existing courses as well as the development of new teaching activities. Teaching and supervision of Bachelor's and Master's thesis students are integral parts of the position, with PhD supervision becoming relevant as the candidate's research activities develop. The position provides an academic career opportunity within a competence-rich and interdisciplinary environment with ambitious and collaborative colleagues and access to research activities spanning electronics, electrical energy technology, biomedical engineering, signal processing, communication, robotics, and related engineering fields. Applicant profile We welcome candidates with a strong background in electronics and particularly encourage applicants with research activities related to sensors, instrumentation, and electronic measurement systems. Relevant areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: •    Sensor technologies, sensor electronics, and instrumentation •    Analog and mixed-signal circuit and system design •    Analog front ends, low-signal electronics, signal conditioning, and data acquisition •    Low-power and energy-efficient electronics •    Embedded electronics, control, and hardware-software integration •    Electronic interfaces and integrated measurement systems •    Electronic system architecture, implementation, testing, and validation We are particularly interested in candidates who can develop and apply novel electronic technologies within healthcare and medical technology. Relevant areas may include biomedical sensors, physiological monitoring, medical instrumentation, wearable technologies, and electronic systems for diagnostics, monitoring, and treatment. We also particularly welcome candidates whose research addresses electronics for energy and industrial applications, including energy conversion, low-power and energy-efficient electronics, sensing, instrumentation and monitoring of electrical and energy systems, power electronic interfaces, embedded control, and intelligent electronic systems. Strong candidates from other application areas within electronics are also encouraged to apply, including robotics and autonomous systems, communication, IoT-enabled instrumentation, and other emerging applications of electronics. Applicants should demonstrate strong fundamental knowledge of electronics and the potential to establish an independent research profile. Experience with experimental research, electronic design and prototyping, system integration, and collaboration with industrial, healthcare, or clinical partners will be considered an advantage. A relevant PhD is required. Postdoctoral research experience or equivalent research and development experience will be considered an advantage. Applicants should demonstrate a strong research record appropriate to their career stage, a clear vision for their future research, and the potential to attract external research funding and establish national and international collaborations. The candidate should be able to contribute to teaching in fundamental electronics, including analog electronics and electronic circuit design. Depending on the candidate's expertise, teaching may also include sensors and instrumentation, low-power analog and digital electronics, mixed-signal systems, embedded electronics, data acquisition, or electronic system design. The candidate must have excellent communication skills and be able to teach and supervise students in English. For non-Danish-speaking applicants, Danish language skills are expected to be developed over a five-year period to enable teaching and supervision in Danish. The applicant should be motivated to collaborate across disciplines, contribute actively to the development of the department's research and educational activities, and contribute positively to the social and professional working environment. Application Your application must be in English and include a Curriculum Vitae including research activities, R&D experience, a complete publication list as well as your teaching and funding experience. Also, a research plan and a teaching plan/statement (length of 2–3 pages each, including your planned future teaching and research directions) are required. Please provide your Researcher ID/Scopus ID, Google Scholar profile link, and/or a unique search query in Web of Science to retrieve your publication and citation track record. Also, please provide names of references (minimum 3) whom the search committee may contact directly. All applications must be made online. The position is available from December 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. Place of Employment and Place of Work The place of employment is Aarhus University, and the place of work is the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Finlandsgade 22, 8200 Aarhus N, Denmark. Further information For more information, please contact Head of Electrical Energy Technology Section Björn Andresen, [email protected] , Head of Biomedical Engineering Section Peter Johansen, [email protected] , or Head of Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering Mikael Bergholz Knudsen, [email protected] Deadline All applications must be made online and received by 15h of October 2026. Technical Sciences Tenure Track Aarhus University offers talented scientists from around the world attractive career perspectives via the Technical Sciences Tenure Track Programme. Highly qualified candidates are appointed as Assistant Professors for a period of six years with the prospect of performance- based advancement to a tenured Associate Professorship. The aim of the Technical Sciences Tenure Track Programme is to: attract outstanding talented individuals that are competitive at an international level to promote the early development of independent research success early in the career of scientists to create transparency in the academic career path As part of the tenure track position, the candidate is offered: access to research infrastructure capability development, including postgraduate teacher training a mentoring programme support to develop scientific networks and to secure interdisciplinary research at the highest level As part of the Aarhus University Tenure Track Programme, the University carries out a mid-way evaluation to review the progress of the tenure track candidate after three years, according to the same criteria used in the final tenure review. The final tenure review is conducted after five and a half years. If the review is positive, the candidate will be offered a tenured position as Associate Professor at Aarhus University. Please refer to the tenure track guidelines for the tenure review criteria and for the tenure review process. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation. The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website . Questions about the position\n                            ? Mikael Bergholz Knudsen Head of Department, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering +4521745355 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4593521962 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday 15 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/research-assistant-within-trapped-ion-quantum-technology": {
      "title": "Research Assistant within Trapped Ion Quantum Technology",
      "description": "Research Assistant within Trapped Ion Quantum Technology - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Physics and Astronomy Research Assistant within Trapped Ion Quantum Technology Deadline 15 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Dec Department of Physics and Astronomy Ny Munkegade 120 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Dec 2026 - 30 Nov 2028 ID: 168436 Apply The Department of Physics and Astronomy (IFA), Aarhus University, Denmark (www.phys.au.dk) invites applications for a 2-year research assistant position with focus on establishing and maintaining trapped ion quantum technology setups. The work will partly be carried out in the newly established Quantum Technology Lab (QTL) and within the Ion Trap Group. The position is open from  the 1 December 2026, or as soon as possible thereafter. Your profile The candidates are expected to have experience in cold trapped ion research, and hence both have experience in ion trap and laser technology. We are seeking enthusiastic candidates with a keen interest in setting up a new teaching ion trap setup in QTL as well as participate in maintaining and running ion trap experiments within the Ion Trap Group. More specifically, the candidate should have a solid background in construction and operating rf ion traps, CW laser systems including control of output powers, polarization states, linewidths and frequencies, as well as an understanding of analog and digital electronics for experimental control. Relevant methodology includes UHV vacuum handling, rf circuity electronics, laser frequency stabilization using optical frequency combs, rubidium atomic clocks, and GPS synchronization. Applicants must be able to document relevant experience, as outlined above. Who we are / The department The Department of Physics and Astronomy is a department at the Faculty of Natural Sciences, Aarhus University, Denmark. The main objectives of the Department are to carry out research at the highest international level, to offer research based teaching at Bachelor of Science, Master of Science and PhD levels, and to exchange knowledge with other areas of society, e.g. by offering our expertise to industrial partners and the business world. The Department is an attractive workplace for researchers, teachers, technicians, laboratory technicians and administrators and has a good study environment with many talented and active students at all levels. The research at the Department of Physics and Astronomy is of high quality, attracts significant international attention – in several fields the Department is within the world elite – and is characterized by many international collaborations with other high-ranking universities and research institutions around the world. The place of work is Ny Munkegade 120, 8000 Aarhus C and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. The successful candidate is offered a working climate inviting lively, open and constructive discussion within and across different fields of research a working environment with teamwork, close working relations, and social activities a workplace characterized by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance a job where the overall environment in Denmark is family-friendly, safe and relaxed – contributing to Denmark consistently being voted amongst the happiest nations in the world. Job description Your tasks include: Establishing and running a novel linear rf ion trap setup for teaching purposes Handling existing CW laser systems including commercial external cavity diode laser systems, TiSa-lasers, fiber-lasers for trapped ion experiments Handling (electro-)optical setups for controlling the frequency and shaping the power level of CW laser Help developing experimental control systems based on the platform ARTIQ (ARTIQ) Contact information Further information can be obtained from Michael Drewsen ( [email protected] ) Deadline The application deadline is the 15 October 2026. Application procedure Short-listing is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation. The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here . Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Michael Drewsen Professor, Department of Physics and Astronomy +4523382354 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday 15 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
