      "title": "Postdoc in GPR6 neurobiology",
      "description": "Postdoc in GPR6 neurobiology - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Clinical Medicine - Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit - Faculty: Health Sciences Postdoc in GPR6 neurobiology Deadline 9 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Dec Department of Clinical Medicine - Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit Palle Juul-Jensens Boulevard 11 8200 Aarhus N Fixed term full-time position 1 Dec 2026 - 30 Nov 2028 ID: 169864 Apply The Department of Clinical Medicine at the Faculty of Health at Aarhus University invites applications for a position as Postdoc in GPR6 neurobiology and translational neuroscience as of 1 December 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is a fixed-term full-time position for two years. As a Postdoc at the Department of Clinical Medicine, you will be part of what is probably the largest health science research department in Denmark. Our clinical research covers all the medical specialities and takes place in close collaboration with the university hospital and the regional hospitals in the Central Denmark Region. We have approx. 30,000 square metres of modern research facilities for experimental surgery and medicine, animal facilities and also advanced scanners at our disposal. The department has overall responsibility for the Master's degree programs in medicine and in molecular medicine. At the department we are approx. 670 academic employees, 500 PhD students and 160 technical/administrative employees who are cooperating across disciplines. As a Postdoc, you will be working at Aarhus University Hospital or another hospital in the Central Denmark Region. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . About the research project The project investigates how G protein-coupled receptor 6 (GPR6) influences striatal signalling, behaviour and responses to pharmacological intervention. We will combine studies in GPR6 knockout mice with pharmacological approaches in rodent models, including Flinders Sensitive Line (FSL) rats, a model of depression. By linking behavioural outcomes to in vivo PET imaging and molecular measures, the project aims to clarify the role of GPR6 in dopamine-related brain function and its potential as a therapeutic target for depression. The project combines behavioural testing with preclinical positron emission tomography (PET), quantitative receptor autoradiography, RNAscope, Western blotting and immunohistochemistry. These complementary methods will relate regional imaging and binding measures to cell-specific gene expression and intracellular signalling. Experience with stress-related behavioural models, particularly chronic social defeat stress, will be valuable for investigating how GPR6 influences stress susceptibility and resilience. You will join the Preclinical Neuroimaging section led by Associate Professor Anne M. Landau at the Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit, Department of Clinical Medicine. The group combines in vivo molecular PET imaging and postmortem autoradiography in translationally relevant animal models of depression and Parkinson’s disease and validates imaging radioligands. You will work closely with colleagues in preclinical imaging, behavioural neuroscience and molecular studies. Your job responsibilities As Postdoc in GPR6 neurobiology, your position is primarily research-based but may also involve teaching assignments. You will contribute to the development of the department through research of high international quality. In your daily work, you will work closely with colleagues on your project, where you will receive supervision and guidance. Your main tasks will consist of: Independent research of high international quality, including publication. Design, conduct, and analyse behavioural experiments in rodent models, including assessments of motor function, affective behaviour, and stress responses. Contribute to preclinical PET studies, including experimental planning, data acquisition in collaboration with imaging colleagues, quantitative analysis and integration with behavioural findings. Perform and develop molecular and tissue-based analyses, including quantitative autoradiography, RNAscope, Western blotting and immunohistochemistry. Maintain rigorous experimental records and apply appropriate randomisation, blinding, statistical analysis and animal welfare procedures. Prepare manuscripts and presentations, collaborate across disciplines and contribute to student supervision and relevant teaching activities. You will report to Associate Professor Anne M. Landau. Your competences You have academic qualifications at PhD level in neuroscience, pharmacology, biomedical science or a related discipline. You have experience in experimental neuroscience, particularly behavioural studies in rodents, and a strong interest in linking behaviour to brain imaging and molecular mechanisms. We are looking for a candidate with: Practical experience in rodent handling, behavioural testing, experimental design and quantitative data analysis. Experience with molecular or histological methods relevant to the project, such as PET, autoradiography, RNAscope or other in situ hybridisation methods, Western blotting and immunohistochemistry. The ability to work independently, document experiments carefully and communicate research findings through scientific publications and presentations. Experience with the chronic social defeat stress model is particularly desirable. Experience with preclinical PET, radioligand binding, genetically modified mouse models or research on dopamine and striatal signalling will be an advantage. Relevant training in laboratory animal science is desirable. As a person, you have good interpersonal skills, are inclusive and team-oriented and able to contribute to a good work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. In order to be assessed as qualified for a Postdoc position, you must meet these academic criteria . To be considered for the position, applicants must meet the formal requirements for academic appointments at Aarhus University and submit all required application materials by the deadline. Applications that are substantially incomplete will not proceed to further assessment Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Associate Professor Anne M. Landau, tel.: (+45) 50209259. Your place of work will be the Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit, Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University, Palle Juul Jensens Boulevard 11, Building A, DK-8200 Aarhus N, Denmark. Terms of employment Appointment as a postdoc requires academic qualifications at PhD level. Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . The yearly base salary for a fulltime postdoc is between DKK 493.394,35 and DKK 585.322,48 depending on the years of working experience after achieved MSc degree. The base salary includes a position related supplement and pension (18.07 %). Additional supplement(s) for special qualifications can be negotiated. Authorisation supplemement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial Order on Job Structure for Academic Staff Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - postdoc A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios A maximum of five of the publications of greatest relevance to the job may be submitted (optional) Research plan can be uploaded (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . Aarhus University also has a Junior Researcher Association and offers career development support. You can read more about these resources here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Anne Landau Associate Professor, Department of Clinical Medicine - Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit +4550209259 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +20748644 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Friday  9 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/lab-technician-in-neurobiology": {
      "title": "Lab technician in neurobiology",
      "description": "Lab technician in neurobiology - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Neurobiology Lab technician in neurobiology Deadline 12 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Neurobiology Universitetsbyen 81 8000 Aarhus C Part-time position 30 hours per week ID: 169810 Apply Department of Molecular Biology & Genetics at Aarhus University is looking for a skilled lab technician for neurobiology. We need a new colleague from January 1st, 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a permanent position from January 1st, 2027 or as soon possible. Job description You will be responsible for culturing primary rat neurons, maintaining the lab supply, reagents, antibody libraries, stock solutions, and routine assays in biochemistry, and cloning You will be working primarily with neuron cultures and brain tissues You will be involved in lab management, purchasing, invoicing and the use of other university admin systems These activities will be in close collaboration with lab members Your profile Experience in primary neuron cultures, cloning, biochemistry, genetics Lab management skills Communication and/or language skills in English and Danish Personal skills Who we are Visit the Sun lab's website to learn more about the lab: https://www.synaptic-logistics-lab.com/ What we offer The department/centre offers: a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a work environment encouraging lively, open and critical discussion a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of work is at Universitetsbyen 81, 8000 Aarhus C, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. Contact information For further information, please contact: Assoc. Professor, Chao Sun, [email protected] Deadline Applications must be received no later than October 12th, 2026. Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Union of Commercial and Clerical Employees in Denmark/State (HK/Stat) for clerical staff, laboratory technicians and IT employees and the joint collective agreement concluded between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Organisations of Public Employees - Denmark - Danish State Sector (the OAO-S joint collective agreement). You will be employed as a Lab technician in salary group 2-6 depending on seniority and qualifications. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Chao Sun Associate Professor, DANDRITE - Sun Group +4593508985 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4530783166 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 12 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
