    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/join-the-nexus-research-academy-as-managing-director": {
      "title": "Join the NEXUS Research Academy as Managing Director",
      "description": "Join the NEXUS Research Academy as Managing Director - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Health Sciences Join the NEXUS Research Academy as Managing Director Deadline 18 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Health Sciences Vennelyst Boulevard 4 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 169893 Apply The Faculty of Health Sciences, Aarhus University invites applications for the position of Managing Director to lead the establishment, development and operation of NEXUS Research Academy — a new national initiative dedicated to advancing cardiometabolic research and strengthening the next generation of research leaders. The starting date for the position is 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. As Managing Director, you will play a central role in shaping NEXUS from its inception, building a solid governance structure and ensuring that the academy becomes a visible platform for research excellence, collaboration and career development across cardiovascular, diabetes and endocrine research. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated experience leading a research organisation, programme, or large portfolio (in a start-up/scale-up phase) ; the ability to engage with and include a broad range of stakeholders; and documented research experience and content expertise within the cardiometabolic field. About NEXUS Research Academy NEXUS Research Academy is a new national initiative designed to unite cardiovascular, diabetes and endocrine research into one coordinated cardiometabolic community with deep Danish roots and Nordic reach. Building on the successful experiences of the Danish Cardiovascular Academy and the Danish Diabetes and Endocrine Academy, NEXUS will support post-PhD early-career researchers through targeted funding, structured talent development and community-building activities across disciplines, sectors and institutions. The ambition of NEXUS is to strengthen Denmark’s position as a leading international hub for cardiometabolic research and innovation. By empowering the next generation of research leaders, fostering collaboration across academia, healthcare, industry and patient organisations, and supporting excellent translational research, NEXUS aims to accelerate scientific breakthroughs and contribute to improved prevention, treatment and health outcomes. NEXUS will be funded by a five-year grant of DKK 149.8m (approx. EUR 20m) from the Novo Nordisk Foundation, potentially supplemented by co-funding. The position is full-time and is not intended to be combined with the candidate’s own research activities or other professional activities. However, a 1-year part-time arrangement may be considered for the right candidate during a transition period. The NEXUS Research Academy is located on the Aarhus University Health Sciences campus. Your job responsibilities As Managing Director, your primary responsibilities will include providing executive operational leadership for NEXUS; implementing the Board-approved strategy; running the secretariat; delivering programmes and grant operations; building partnerships; ensuring sound financial and organisational management; and maintaining alignment with the application submitted to the Novo Nordisk Foundation. Your main tasks will consist of: Implementing the NEXUS Board-approved strategy and translating it into annual operational plans and deliverables Leading the Secretariat, including people management, roles, workflows, and organisational culture Ensuring effective delivery of NEXUS’ core activities (talent development, community-building, and postdoctoral fellowships) through operational execution and quality control Managing finances and operations, including budget oversight, resource allocation, and compliance in collaboration with the host institution Managing governance interfaces, including preparing Board matters, supporting committees/fora, and ensuring decisions are executed Developing and maintaining strategic partnerships across academia, healthcare, industry, patient organisations, and Nordic/international collaborators Representing NEXUS externally, ensuring visibility, stakeholder alignment, and credibility in the cardiometabolic community Being the point-of-contact to grantees. The Managing Director will be employed by the Administration Centre at the Faculty of Health Sciences, Aarhus University. Day-to-day management will be handled by the Administration Centre Manager, while strategic priorities, Board-related activities and obligations linked to the Novo Nordisk Foundation grant will be coordinated with the Chairperson of the NEXUS Board of Directors . Your profile We are looking for a candidate with a background in life science research and/or research management within cardiovascular, diabetes and/or endocrine research. You bring solid experience in leading complex initiatives with multiple stakeholders and are able to translate Board-level strategy into clear operational priorities and tangible results. You understand how to work effectively with boards, committees, internal and external stakeholders, and you bring high integrity in decision-making. Experience with arm’s-length principles, conflict-of-interest management, competitive funding calls and peer-review processes will be important in this role. You bring experience in developing and delivering activities that support research talent, collaboration and community-building — for example through courses, summits, networks or fellowship programmes. You are also able to build partnerships across universities, hospitals, industry, patient organisations and Nordic or international collaborators. The role requires solid financial and operational management skills, including budget oversight, resource allocation and collaboration with HR and finance functions at the host institution. You communicate clearly and credibly with researchers, clinicians, funders and external stakeholders, and you can represent NEXUS externally while helping to build a clear shared identity for the academy. As a person, you are inclusive, collaborative and able to create clarity and momentum in complex settings. You listen well, bring people together, value humor and a good laugh and take ownership with a structured and solution-oriented approach. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written Danish and English. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Dean of Health Sciences, Anne-Mette Hvas, tel.: (+45) 8715 2007. Your place of work will be located at Aarhus University Health Sciences campus , DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. We expect to conduct interviews in week 43 (1st round) and in week 46 (2nd round). You may be given a case in connection with the interviews and if you proceed to 2nd round you will be expected to complete an assessment test (NEO-PI-3). Members from the interim board of directors of NEXUS will be part of the appointment committee. Terms of employment Terms of employment and pay are regulated by the collective agreement between the Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Indication of education (a copy of the diplomas should be uploaded) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the recruitment system We refer to the faculty’s Guideline for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Anne-Mette Hvas Dean, Deans Office, Health +4587152007 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4593508308 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday 18 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/administrative-research-theme-coordinator": {
      "title": "Administrative Research Theme Coordinator",
      "description": "Administrative Research Theme Coordinator - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biomedicine - Sekretariat, Syd Administrative Research Theme Coordinator Deadline 20 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biomedicine - Sekretariat, Syd Wilhelm Meyers Allé 3 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 170027 Apply The Department of Biomedicine at the Faculty of Health Sciences at Aarhus University invites applications for a position of Administrative Research Theme Coordinator, starting on 1 January 2027. The position is a permanent, full-time position. The Department of Biomedicine prioritizes diversity and a good work environment, as these are prerequisites for groundbreaking research. In a diverse and international research environment, our dedicated employees work to generate new knowledge within biomedical research. The Department of Biomedicine provides research-based teaching of the highest quality and is responsible for a large part of the medical degree programme. Academic staff contribute to the teaching. English is the preferred language in the laboratory, at meetings, and at seminars. The department employs approx. 600 people from all over the world, and they make use of the department's modern laboratory, core, and animal facilities. The Department of Biomedicine focuses on innovation, entrepreneurship, and collaboration with business and industry, and numerous researchers from the department have established companies to develop new medicinal treatments founded in professional scientific basic research. Your job responsibilities As Administrative Research Theme Coordinator your main responsibility is to provide administrative support to the four Research Theme Coordinators and help coordinate activities across the four research themes at the department. You will work closely with each of the Research Theme Coordinators to plan activities, coordinate tasks, follow up on agreed actions, and help with the day-to-day running and development of the themes. An important part of the role is to stay on top of what is happening in the themes and anticipate where support or coordination may be needed. You take initiatives, follow tasks through, and make it easier for the Research Theme Coordinators and theme members to get things done. You will also help coordinate activities across themes and ensure relevant information is shared with the department. Your main tasks will include: Support the Research Theme Coordinators in planning and running academic and social activities. Coordinate annual theme meetings/retreats and other theme activities. Coordinate international seminars, including the themes’ annual Skou Lectures, as well as other seminars and events. Help onboarding of new theme members and keep mailing lists, theme websites and other practical information up to date. Support the activities of the theme-associated young networks, when necessary Help planning and preparation of theme meetings and follow up on agreed-upon actions. Support department-wide activities, including Department Day, and coordinate the themes’ involvement. You will work closely with the four Research Theme Coordinators, the department’s management consultant, and other relevant administrative and academic staff in the department or at the faculty. In the Administrative Research Theme Coordinator function, you will be responsible to the Research Theme Coordinators with a formal reference to Head of Department. Your competences We are looking for someone with a relevant academic background and solid knowledge of and experience from an academic research environment. As a person, you enjoy a busy and outgoing role with many different tasks and contacts across the department. You are curious about people and interested in understanding different perspectives. You are aware of how you communicate and able to adapt your approach to different roles and situations. Engagement and dedication are important in this role. You enjoy making things happen and helping others. You are organized and reliable, and you are comfortable working independently while knowing when to involve others. You communicate well with both academic and administrative colleagues, and we expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. We offer A vibrant and diverse workplace with a high degree of freedom and room for new ideas, opinions, and ways of doing things. A safe and respectful working environment, where everyone contributes to making it a good place to work. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of Department Thomas G. Jensen tel.: (+45) 2778 2805. Your place of work will be the Department of Biomedicine, Høegh-Guldbergs Gade 10, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. We expect to conduct interviews in November. If you are invited for a second interview, you may be asked to prepare a case as part of the interview process . Terms of employment Terms of employment and pay are regulated by the collective agreement between the Ministry of Taxation and Academics in the State. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8 or as Special Consultant. Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Indication of education (a copy of the diplomas should be uploaded) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the recruitment system We refer to the faculty’s Guideline for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Thomas G. Jensen Professor, Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Skou-bygningen +4527782805 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4521623283 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 20 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/2-year-postdoc-position-in-soil-sensing": {
      "title": "2-year Postdoc position in soil sensing",
      "description": "2-year Postdoc position in soil sensing - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Microbiology 2-year Postdoc position in soil sensing Deadline 31 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biology - Microbiology Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 169765 Apply Are you interested in optical sensors and ammonia (NH3) emissions? Then you might want to contribute to the BholdN project where we want to make NH3 emissions visible in biochar treated fields. We offer a 2-year postdoc position in the field of optical sensing at the Department of Biology, Aarhus University, Denmark Expected start date and duration of employment This is a 2-year position from 1.1.2027 or as soon as possible. Job description You will be contributing to/establishing/developing an in-situ imaging system for NH3. You will be further developing optical NH3 sensors in the laboratory and in the field. You will be involved in field campaigns. These activities will be in close collaboration with the project partners from Copenhagen University and the Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering from AU. Your profile Applicants must hold a PhD in Biology, Chemistry or similar fields. Applicants are expected to have: Documented experience in optical sensing (requirement) Publication experience in leading discipline or interdisciplinary journals Collaboration skills Communication skills with different stakeholders Danish language skills are an asset as the work also includes working with local stakeholders High level of self-motivation. Who we are The Sensor Laboratory at the Department of Biology is internationally recognized for the development and application of advanced chemical sensing technologies. We have pioneered several innovative approaches to chemical sensing and imaging for environmental and biological applications. Led by Prof. Klaus Koren and Associate Prof. Ugo Marzocchi, the laboratory combines fundamental sensor development with real-world applications in complex biological systems, from soils and sediments to microbial communities and aquatic environments. The laboratory currently hosts three PhD students and six postdoctoral researchers, bringing together an interdisciplinary team with backgrounds spanning chemistry, biology, physics, and engineering. This combination of expertise enables us to develop new sensing technologies and translate them into tools for investigating chemical processes in complex, heterogeneous environments What we offer The department/laboratory offers: A well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment An exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators A research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research A work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities A workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Ny Munkegade 114, 8000 Aarhus C, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. Contact information For further information, please contact Prof. Klaus Koren, [email protected] Deadline Applications must be received no later than 31.10.2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Klaus Koren Professor, Department of Biology - Microbiology +4531500741 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520968189 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Saturday 31 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-researcher-in-biodiversity-forecasting-vegetation-dynamics-and-rewilding": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral researcher in biodiversity forecasting, vegetation dynamics and rewilding",
      "description": "Postdoctoral researcher in biodiversity forecasting, vegetation dynamics and rewilding - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Postdoctoral researcher in biodiversity forecasting, vegetation dynamics and rewilding Deadline 1 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2029 ID: 169869 Apply Are you interested in developing spatially explicit forecasts of how rural depopulation, climate and land-use change, and alternative rewilding pathways may reshape plant diversity and ecosystem functioning across Europe? The Department of Biology at Aarhus University invites applications for a two-year postdoctoral position, offering an exciting opportunity to join the new Biodiversa+ REDESIGN project. Expected start date and duration of employment 1 January 2027 or as soon possible. Job description The postdoctoral researcher will focus on modelling how depopulation and rewilding impact on biodiversity & ecosystem structure under rising biosphere novelty. The work will integrate ecological, demographic, environmental and remote-sensing information to develop scenario-based forecasts of European vegetation and biodiversity dynamics. The main tasks will be to: assemble, harmonise and analyse large spatial and temporal biodiversity datasets, including vegetation-plot, environmental, land-use, demographic and remote-sensing data; develop spatially explicit forecasts of plant diversity and community change under alternative climate, socioeconomic, land-use, depopulation and rewilding scenarios, with projections extending to 2100 and, where appropriate, 2300; develop and/or integrate hierarchical species distribution models and/or dynamic vegetation modelling approaches, using contemporary and historical biodiversity data for calibration and evaluation; quantify uncertainty and conduct sensitivity analyses to determine the relative influence of climatic, ecological and socioeconomic drivers on projected biodiversity change; assess the functional consequences of projected community change by linking vegetation forecasts to plant functional traits, ecosystem structure, productivity, carbon storage, nutrient cycling, functional redundancy, resilience and other indicators of ecological integrity; compare trophic rewilding and passive-management trajectories, including the potential effects of large-herbivore and carnivore distributions or reintroductions in land-abandonment landscapes; identify potential refugia, ecological traps and areas with high potential for biodiversity recovery or rewilding under future environmental change; produce reproducible geospatial workflows, scenario maps, scientific publications and material contributing to decision-support tools and policy-oriented outputs; and collaborate closely with project partners across Europe and contribute to the synthesis and communication of the project results. The position provides substantial scope for methodological development and scientific publication at the interface of macroecology, biodiversity modelling, global-change ecology and restoration science. Your profile Applicants must hold a PhD or have submitted a PhD thesis before the starting date, in ecology, macroecology, biogeography, environmental science, geography, ecological modelling, ecoinformatics or a closely related field. We are particularly interested in candidates with several of the following qualifications: advanced quantitative abilities and proven experience in spatial ecological modeling experience with species distribution models, joint/hierarchical community models, dynamic vegetation models, or related biodiversity forecasting approaches; advanced programming skills in R and/or Python and experience with reproducible analytical workflows; experience handling large geospatial, biodiversity, vegetation-plot, remote-sensing or environmental datasets; knowledge of climate and land-use scenarios, including SSP/RCP-based projections, and methods for propagating or quantifying model uncertainty; experience analysing plant community composition, functional traits, functional diversity, ecosystem functioning or ecological stability; experience with remote-sensing products and cloud/geospatial computing is an advantage; a publication record that is competitive for the candidate’s career stage, along with the ability to present findings clearly through scientific writing and visualizations; and the capacity to work on one’s own while making meaningful contributions to an interdisciplinary, international research team Proficiency in both written and spoken English is required. Who we are The Department of Biology ( Department of Biology - Aarhus University ) provides a framework for research and teaching in all major biological subdisciplines. The department is especially known for its research contribution in biodiversity, global change and conservation biology, aquatic biology and ecology, arctic environment and ecosystems, genetics and evolution, microbiology and zoophysiology. The department currently encompasses approximately 200 academic and technical staff and 45 PhD students while 400 students follow the BSc and MSc programs offered by the institute. The position will be Embeded with the ECONOVO research center.  The primary mission of ECONOVO is to provide the scientific foundation needed to steer emerging, novel environmental conditions toward positive outcomes that sustain global biodiversity and keep the planet livable. The Department offers a vibrant and informal research environment with a long-standing tradition of collaboration with international university partners. Much of the internal communication is in English and we welcome international applicants. What we offer an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international collaborators a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of employment is Aarhus University, Department of Biology, Section for Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity (ECOINF), Ny Munkegade 114, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. The work involves some periods of travel away from Aarhus for project meetings. Contact information For further information, please contact: Associate professor,  Alejandro Ordonez via mail ( [email protected] ). Deadline Applications must be received no later than 1 November 2026. Questions about the position\n                            ? Alejandro Ordonez Gloria Associate Professor, Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity +4587154347 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520612514 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday  1 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/lab-engineer-in-mechanical-engineering-1": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Lab Engineer in Mechanical Engineering (re-advertisement) - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Mechanical and Production Engineering - Workshop, MPE Lab Engineer in Mechanical Engineering (re-advertisement) Deadline 5 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Mechanical and Production Engineering - Workshop, MPE Katrinebjergvej 89C 8200 Aarhus N Full-time position ID: 170084 Apply This vacancy is a re-advertisement of a previously posted position. Please note that the role description and the candidate profile have been significantly revised, and applicants are encouraged to review the updated requirements carefully. Aarhus University is seeking a skilled Lab Engineer to support research and teaching activities in the Department of Mechanical and Production Engineering. The position is a full-time permanent appointment from 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. In this role, you will provide technical support for research-based laboratory work and contribute to the operation, development, and maintenance of laboratory infrastructure across the department. The position involves collaboration with researchers, students, and technical staff in a broad range of experimental activities within mechanical and production engineering. The successful candidate will contribute to the planning, operation, and development of experimental setups and laboratory facilities, including advanced measurement systems, instrumentation, and data acquisition solutions. Expected start date and duration of employment The position is a full-time permanent appointment from 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description Operation, maintenance, calibration, and troubleshooting of laboratory equipment and experimental infrastructure. Support for the development, implementation, and commissioning of new experimental setups, instrumentation, and measurement systems for research and teaching activities, in collaboration with our prototype workshop. Assistance with laboratory testing, sensor integration and synchronization, data acquisition, technical documentation, and handling of experimental research data. Support for sensor-based measurement systems, including force, acceleration, pressure, temperature, and flow-related instrumentation where relevant and the necessary signal conditioning and data acquisition. Contribution to laboratory safety procedures, user support, and training of students and staff. Support for research projects and student theses and occasional lab exercises in relation to teaching efforts. Your profile Applicants must hold an MSc degree in Mechanical Engineering or a related discipline. Hands-on experience with laboratory infrastructure, instrumentation, and experimental methods is required. Experience with measurement systems, sensor integration, calibration, and data acquisition is required. Strong written and oral communication skills in English and the ability to work effectively with students, researchers, and technical staff are required. Experience with experimental data analysis, laboratory control systems, or software such as LabVIEW is an advantage. Familiarity with force, acceleration, pressure, temperature, or flow measurements will be considered a strength. Experience from research projects, research-oriented laboratory environments, or a PhD degree will be considered a plus. Who we are The candidate will become part of our workshop and laboratory unit and will have 8 colleagues, two of whom will perform similar functions. The Department of Mechanical and Production Engineering at Aarhus University is theoretically and experimentally oriented, with an emphasis on research and teaching in advanced manufacturing, robotics, and mechanical systems. The department is involved in a wide range of projects with both academic and industrial partners, including robots, renewable energy systems, and advanced manufacturing systems. Department of Mechanical & Production Engineering - Aarhus University What we offer a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a work environment encouraging lively, open and critical discussion a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Katrinebjergvej 89C, 8200 Aarhus N Aarhus, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. Contact information For further information, please contact Deputy Head of Department for research Charles Møller ( [email protected] , +45 61677786) or Head of Workshops and Laboratories Peter Lykke ( [email protected] , +45 41893325). Deadline Applications must be received no later than 5. November 2026. Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Peter Harling Lykke Head of Workshops and laboratories, Department of Mechanical and Production Engineering - Workshop, MPE +4541893325 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522140468 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday  5 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/phd-special-call-phd-position-in-ai-and-workplace-inclusion-msca-doctoral-network-emanaire": {
      "title": "PhD special call: PhD position in AI and Workplace Inclusion",
      "description": "PhD special call: PhD position in AI and Workplace Inclusion – MSCA Doctoral Network EMANAIRE - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Management PhD special call: PhD position in AI and Workplace Inclusion – MSCA Doctoral Network EMANAIRE Deadline 1 Dec 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Feb Department of Management Universitetsbyen 61 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Feb 2027 - 31 Jan 2030 ID: 170141 Apply Aarhus BSS Graduate School, Aarhus University, invites applications for a PhD scholarship in connection with the EU-funded Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network EMANAIRE (Empowering Human Agency in AI-Augmented Futures of Work) at the PhD Programme in Management, Department of Management. The position is available from 1 February 2027 and is a full-time, 36-month position. The research project EMANAIRE is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network bringing together 15 doctoral candidates at nine European universities to study how AI is changing work. The network examines the implications of generative and agentic AI for areas such as leadership, everyday work practices, team communication, professional learning, careers, and inclusion. Doctoral candidates become part of an international research and training network involving universities and practice partners across Europe. The PhD project at Aarhus University focuses on AI and workplace inclusion. AI is increasingly used in organizational practices such as recruitment, evaluation, communication, coordination, learning, and career development. These developments may create new opportunities for workplace inclusion, while also potentially reproducing existing inequalities or generating new forms of exclusion. The overall aim of the PhD project is to develop new knowledge about how the introduction and use of AI in organizations may influence inclusion and exclusion at work. Of particular interest are the ways in which AI may shape interpersonal work relations and interactions and thereby influence employees’ opportunities to participate, contribute, learn, develop, and pursue careers in organizational settings. Job description Applicants are invited to develop their own PhD project within the broad theme of AI and workplace inclusion. The proposed project should formulate a theoretically and practically relevant research problem concerning how AI may support or constrain inclusion at work. Possible topics include the influence of AI on interpersonal work relations and interaction affecting issues such as belongingness, authenticity, employee participation, visibility, learning and development, or career opportunities. The project may examine the use of AI in different organizational practices and settings and may focus on employees, managers, teams, or other relevant organizational actors. Projects may examine inclusion in relation to, for example, ethnicity, cultural or linguistic background, gender, age, disability, or other relevant dimensions of diversity. These are examples rather than predefined target groups, and applicants are not expected to examine all or any particular combination of these groups. Applicants are encouraged to develop their own theoretical perspective, empirical focus, and methodological approach within the overall framework of the project. Depending on the research question, possible methods could include qualitative fieldwork, interviews, case studies, surveys, experiments, or other relevant forms of data collection and analysis. Applicants are required to submit a project description outlining how they would develop and conduct their research within the overall theme of AI and workplace inclusion and the objectives of EMANAIRE. The selected candidate’s exact research questions, theoretical perspective, empirical focus, and methodological approach will depend on the candidate’s proposed project and will subsequently be further developed in collaboration with the supervisory team. As part of EMANAIRE, the selected candidate will participate in international research and training activities, including network schools, seminars, workshops, and collaboration with other doctoral candidates and researchers across the network. The position also involves international academic mobility and a secondment of at least three months with a practice partner. The secondment is intended to connect the candidate’s research with organizational practice through mutual exchange of knowledge, ideas, and insights relevant to the PhD project. Your qualifications We are looking for a candidate with an interest in AI, digitalization, organizations, and workplace inclusion who wishes to develop an original PhD project within this broad research area. Applicants should hold, or expect to complete before the start of the position, a relevant Master’s degree or equivalent that meets the admission requirements of Aarhus BSS Graduate School. Relevant academic backgrounds may include management, organization studies, HRM, international business, psychology, sociology, information systems, or related fields. Applicants with other academic backgrounds are also encouraged to apply if their qualifications and proposed project are relevant to the research area. We are particularly interested in candidates who can formulate a clear, original, and feasible research problem, engage critically with relevant theory and research, and develop a methodological approach appropriate to their research question. Experience with specific methods or particular groups of employees is not a requirement. Candidates should also be interested in participating actively in an international doctoral network and in engaging with academic and practice partners during the PhD. MSCA eligibility requirements Applicants may be of any nationality. In addition to the admission requirements of Aarhus BSS Graduate School, candidates must meet the eligibility requirements of the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Networks. At the time of recruitment, applicants must not hold a doctoral degree. Applicants must also comply with the MSCA mobility rule: they must not have resided or carried out their main activity, such as work or studies, in Denmark for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding the date of recruitment. Compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent as part of a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention are not taken into account. The appointment is conditional on documentary verification of the MSCA eligibility requirements and fulfillment of Aarhus University’s requirements for admission to the PhD programme. Research environment and supervision The PhD project will be based at the Department of Management, Aarhus BSS, Aarhus University, and will also draw on research expertise from the Department of Psychology and Behavioural Sciences. The Department of Management conducts internationally oriented research and teaching within areas including management and organization, international and cross-cultural management, organizational behavior and HRM, digitalization, and the changing organization of work. The Department of Psychology and Behavioural Sciences conducts research across major areas of psychology, including work and organizational psychology, learning, social processes, and the implications of digital technologies for work and human interaction. The project thereby offers an interdisciplinary research environment combining expertise in international and cross-cultural management, work and organizational psychology, workplace inclusion, organizational learning, digitalization, virtual collaboration, and AI-supported work. The PhD project will be supervised by Professor Jakob Lauring, Department of Management, Aarhus University; Associate Professor Charlotte Jonasson, Department of Psychology and Behavioural Sciences, Aarhus University; and Professor Katja Einola, Stockholm School of Economics. The international collaboration within EMANAIRE provides the candidate with access to a broader European research network working on AI and the changing world of work. Further information For further information about the academic content of the position, please contact Professor Jakob Lauring, Department of Management, Aarhus University, e-mail: [email protected] . If you need assistance with uploading your application or have questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Aarhus BSS HR & PhD at [email protected] . Place of work Department of Management, Aarhus BSS, Aarhus University, Universitetsbyen 61–63, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Assessment Criteria Applications are assessed based on an overall evaluation of the applicant’s qualifications. Particular emphasis is placed on: the quality, originality, and relevance of the research proposal academic performance in both bachelor’s and master’s degree studies evidence of a demonstrated ability to produce substantial written work, such as a master’s thesis, during studies or in other contexts The final decision regarding admission is made by the management based on an assessment of both the quality of the proposed project and the applicant’s ability to complete it within the prescribed timeframe. Applicants are encouraged to include documentation of their class ranking (e.g. ranking or percentile), as this provides additional context for the assessment. However, this is not a requirement. Framework for the PhD Programme The PhD programme is conducted in accordance with the current Danish Ministerial Order on the PhD Programme and the rules and guidelines for PhD students at Aarhus BSS, both of which can be found here . It is not essential that your educational background matches the PhD programme exactly. The most important consideration is that your project is academically relevant and can be anchored within the research area of the PhD programme. How to apply You can read more about the application requirements in the application guide and find answers to frequently asked questions concerning the application process in our FAQ . What do we offer? Aarhus BSS is part of Aarhus University, an internationally recognised research university with well-established academic environments and close engagement with society and industry. As a PhD student, you will gain: access to renowned researchers and international networks an engaging and supportive research environment a pathway to a research career or a career in the private or public sector We are committed to creating an inclusive environment and recognize diversity as a key asset. We encourage all qualified candidates to apply. Formal Requirements You can read more about how to apply in the application guide and find the rules and regulations of the PhD education here . The following criteria are decisive in the assessment of the candidates: the applicant’s academic qualifications (assessed on the basis of the qualifying degree) the quality and relevance of the project proposal the applicant’s level of academic aptitude and assessed ability to complete the project within the framework of the PhD programme You can read more about the assessment procedure and committees appointed by the dean here . Applicants are very much encouraged to supply documentation as to how they rank compared to their class (both Bachelor’s and Master’s degrees). This may for example be in the form of a letter from your University stating either how you rank as an individual student compared to other students the year you graduated (\"ranks as no. 6 out of 111\" or \"ranks as among the best 8 percent\") or if that is not possible, general information from your University as to how grades were distributed in the year of your graduation, will be appreciated. Please note that Aarhus BSS Graduate School reserves the right to evaluate the applications on the basis of the documentation received before deadline, and you will not necessarily be asked for further documentation. Furthermore, Aarhus BSS Graduate School reserves the right to reject application that do not comply with the limitations as stated in the application guide (see link above). Terms of enrolment and employment PhD students at Aarhus BSS Graduate School are always enrolled at one of the six PhD programmes. It is not a prerequisite for admission to a PhD program to have an educational background within the specific program. What matters is that the PhD project is relevant to the program. Relevant professional experience may also serve as an additional qualification when assessing applicants. The enrolment happens in accordance with the Ministerial PhD Order no. 1124 of 19 September, 2025 and the terms stated in the Rules and Regulations for the PhD programmes at Aarhus BSS Graduate School. Both can be found here . Salary and employment terms are governed by the collective agreement for academics employed by the Danish state . You will be appointed as a PhD fellow at base pay level 4–8, depending on your educational background and qualifications. The appointment is made in accordance with the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities as well as the circular on the Collective Agreement for Academics Employed by the State (in Danish) . The job content and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . You can read more about the structure of the PhD education here . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Aarhus University Aarhus University is an academically diverse and research-intensive university with a strong commitment to high-quality research and education and the development of society nationally and globally. The university offers an inspiring research and teaching environment to its 37,000 students (FTEs) and 8,700 employees and has an annual revenue of EUR 1.106 billion. Learn more at www.international.au.dk/ Questions about the position\n                            ? Jakob Lauring Professor, Department of Management +4593508317 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Aarhus BSS Administrative Centre - Aarhus BSS HR and PhD Administration +4587152397 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday  1 Dec 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/associate-professorships-in-economics-and-business-economics-7": {
      "title": "Associate Professorships in Economics and Business Economics",
      "description": "Associate Professorships in Economics and Business Economics - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Economics and Business Economics - Aarhus BSS Associate Professorships in Economics and Business Economics Deadline 1 Dec 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Aug Department of Economics and Business Economics Universitetsbyen 51 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 170236 Apply The Department of Economics and Business Economics at Aarhus BSS, Aarhus University, invites applications for a number of positions as associate professor in the field of economics and business economics. The associate professorship is a full-time, permanent position with expected starting date 1 August 2027 or as soon as possible subject to mutual agreement. The department conducts research and teaching within economics and business economics covering fields such as microeconomics, macroeconomics, finance, econometrics, business intelligence and operations research. Job description As associate professor you are expected to contribute significantly to the research, teaching and general academic environment of the department and to promote the overall strategy of the department and Aarhus BSS. You are expected to teach and supervise students at Bachelor’s, Master’s and PhD level and to carry out research of the highest international standards, which contributes to developing the academic field of economics and business economics. Also, you are expected to attract external research funding, to develop public and/or business relations, to contribute to knowledge exchange and dissemination of research results, to take on relevant administrative duties, and to contribute constructively to the academic and collegial environment of the department. The department is international and has more than 30 nationalities represented as department members. Non-Danish applicants are expected to learn Danish within the first few years of employment. Your qualifications To qualify for an associate professorship, you must demonstrate research excellence and momentum, strong teaching qualifications, and good academic and collegial citizenship. You have a well-documented international research profile, with publications in leading and well-respected peer-reviewed journals in the relevant field. As a guideline, you should document approximately 3–6 publications in good general-interest or good field journals, and a productivity equivalent to at least one publication per research year. Top general-interest or top field journals carry greater weight and generally quality and impact are preferred to and compensates for quantity. Your research pipeline should demonstrate continued momentum. Working papers, papers submitted to journals, and papers documented to be at the revise-and-resubmit stage may also be taken into consideration. You should demonstrate increasing research independence and a variety of research contributions and co-author constellations, and you should have begun establishing national and international research networks. Equally important, you must demonstrate high-quality teaching and supervision at Bachelor's and/or Master's level. Experience with course responsibility, including curriculum development and the development of course material, is preferable but not a requirement. You must have completed the pedagogical course for assistant professors at Aarhus BSS or obtained an equivalent qualification. Teaching qualifications will be assessed on the basis of the teaching portfolio, with emphasis on teaching evaluations, experience with different forms of teaching, and commitment to teaching and supervision. Experience with, or potential for, attracting external research funding, outreach and knowledge exchange, and contributions to a good academic and collegial work environment are considered important qualifications in addition to research and teaching. Permanent employment as associate professor at Aarhus BSS requires completion of the supervision course and also the course Online and blended learning provided by Centre for Educational Development at Aarhus University. If you have not completed the courses before commencement of employment, continued employment will be conditional on completion of the courses within a two-year period. Who we are At the Department of Economics and Business Economics, we aim to conduct research and teaching of the highest quality and impact. We believe that a supportive atmosphere and appreciative work environment are fundamental for our work and professional development. The department employs approximately 140 academic staff members and 55 PhD students, and our academic staff is highly international and culturally diverse. We focus on the recruitment of excellent national and international researchers. Our research and degree programmes have a strong international focus. The Department hosts a number of research centers and external funding for research activities has top priority. Read more about the department on our website www.econ.au.dk The Department of Economics and Business Economics is one of the six departments at Aarhus BSS, one of the five faculties at Aarhus University. Aarhus BSS unites the business and social science-related research fields and brings them closer together to reflect the close relationship between society and the business community. As part of a Top 100 university, Aarhus BSS has achieved the triple-crown AACSB, AMBA and EQUIS accreditations. Further information For further information about the position, please contact Head of Department, Professor Thomas Quistgaard Pedersen, tel.: +45 27467467, e-mail: [email protected] If you need help uploading your application or have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR supporter Ulla Prisholm Bjørn, e-mail: [email protected] . Place of work Aarhus University, Universitetsbyen 51, 8000 Aarhus C. Are you an international applicant? Aarhus University offers a wide range of services for international academic staff and their accompanying families, including relocation support and career counselling for accompanying partners. Find more information here: Relocation Service at Aarhus University Learn more about research opportunities at Aarhus University here: Aarhus University research Terms of employment The appointment is made in accordance with the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities as well as the circular on the Collective Agreement for Academics Employed by the State (in Danish) . The job content and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . Salary and terms of employment are in accordance with the collective agreement for academics in the state sector. You will be employed as associate professor at basic salary level 4–8, with supplements in accordance with the protocol on certain terms of employment for academic staff at universities. Application procedure When you apply for this position it is mandatory to attach the following: Cover letter. You are encouraged to elaborate on your current and future research plans and on how you match the job description and the required qualifications. Curriculum Vitae. You are encouraged to declare any periods of leave without research activity, including, for example, maternity leave, since your research activities are assessed in relation to your actual research time. Education. Diploma for master's, PhD and possibly higher doctoral degree. List of publications, working papers and work-in-progress. The enclosed papers (see bullet below) must be clearly marked on the list. The list should contain publication status and abstracts for non-enclosed papers. Publications. Up to five papers, including both publications and working papers, can be included in the application. In the event of co-authors the included papers must be accompanied by a co-author statement concerning your share of the collaborative work with the consent of the co-authors. This template may be used for the purpose. Teaching portfolio. The specific requirements regarding the documentation can be found here Materials which cannot be uploaded together with the application may be submitted in five copies to Aarhus BSS HR & PhD, Aarhus University, Bartholins Allé 16, DK-8000 Aarhus C. Read more about how to apply for an academic post at Aarhus BSS here . The evaluation process Shortlisting is applied. This means that an appointment committee (assisted by the chair of the assessment committee) advises the head of department on which applicants possess the qualifications, competencies, experience and potential described above under “Your qualifications”, notably in fields with specific departmental research and teaching needs and in accordance with the faculty’s criteria and guidelines for evaluating candidates to permanent positions. Through the shortlisting process, the head of department selects the applicants who will receive a thorough assessment of their professional qualifications. The applicants will then be assessed by an expert assessment committee. All applicants will be notified whether or not their application has been shortlisted and forwarded to the assessment committee for evaluation. Shortlisted applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee. Please note that the assessment of applicants is based solely on the material received prior to the application deadline. Read more about the recruitment process, shortlisting procedure and guidelines for assessment committees here . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Thomas Quistgaard Pedersen Head of Department, Department of Economics and Business Economics +4527467467 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Aarhus BSS Administrative Centre - Aarhus BSS HR and PhD Administration +4593521364 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday  1 Dec 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
