    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-in-reimagining-brainbow-for-extracellular-vesicle-bioimaging": {
      "title": "Postdoc in Reimagining Brainbow for Extracellular Vesicle Bioimaging",
      "description": "Postdoc in Reimagining Brainbow for Extracellular Vesicle Bioimaging - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Molecular Health Postdoc in Reimagining Brainbow for Extracellular Vesicle Bioimaging Deadline 28 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Molecular Health Universitetsbyen 81 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 170073 Apply Are you passionate about bioimaging and ready to tackle the innovative challenge of engineering rainbow-coloured extracellular vesicles? Then, the Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics, Aarhus University, invites you to apply for a 2-year postdoc position. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a 2-year position from 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description The project is funded by Villum Foundation through the Villum Experiment grant From Genesis to Terminus: Rainbow-Painting of Extracellular Vesicles for Single-Particle Tracking . As a postdoc, you will primarily use cell cultures, and zebrafish embryos as a model organism, with the ambitious aim of developing innovative approaches in extracellular vesicle bioimaging. Through the project, you will be trained as an expert in the extracellular vesicle field with skills in genetic engineering of extracellular vesicles (including transient/stable transgenesis of zebrafish), live embryo imaging, and super-resolution imaging of cells. Your main tasks will consist of: Research of high international quality and publication in top journals. Collaboration with colleagues who work on extracellular vesicles and advanced bioimaging. Research-based supervision of Bachelor, Master and PhD students in a vibrant group. Your profile We are looking for a highly skilled and motivated person who are ready to take a risk and gain high through the Experiment grant. The candidate must have: A PhD degree in a relevant field (e.g. Molecular Biology, Cell Biology). A proven track record either in advanced bioimaging or extracellular vesicle biology. The successful candidate will have one or more of the followings: Expertise in super-resolution microscopy. Expertise in confocal/light-sheet microscopy of zebrafish embryos and image analysis. Experience with genetic engineering (plasmid construction and transfection). Experience in working with zebrafish or other animal models. Finally, we look for a candidate with: A high motivation and ambition in pursuing an academic career path. A strong collaborative spirit for working with colleagues and engaging with students. Excellent skills in communication and presentation. Fluency in spoken and written English. Who we are You will join a vibrant, collaborative, and international research community, where English is the primary language used for internal communication and teaching, and international candidates are not required to learn Danish. Aarhus University offers a wide range of services to support international researchers and their families, including career counselling for accompanying partners. You will be affiliated with an active research group led by Yuya Hayashi at the Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics. Yuya started his own group on extracellular vesicle biology and RNA nanotechnology funded by a Hallas-Møller Emerging Investigator grant from Novo Nordisk Foundation in 2022. Using the zebrafish as a model organism, the team focuses on studying the role of extracellular vesicles in cell-to-cell communication as inspiration for new nanomedicine designs based on the innovative \"RNA Origami\" technology. Visit his group webpage ( http://mbg.au.dk/yuya-hayashi/ ) for further information. Yuya also organises a cross-departmental network of extracellular vesicle researchers at Aarhus University, EVAnet ( http://mbg.au.dk/evanet/ ). What we offer The department offers: A well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment. An exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators. A research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research. A work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities. A workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Universitetsbyen 81, 8000 Aarhus C, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. The city of Aarhus In Aarhus you have easy access to beautiful nature, an exciting culture and city life as well as a safe environment for children - a great place for the whole family. The city of Aarhus has everything you need within a surprisingly small area: exciting national and international jobs, delightful residential areas, a rich cultural life, and beautiful surrounding landscape of woods and coastline that make Aarhus a wonderful place to live and work. See “life in Denmark” at Aarhus and surroundings (au.dk) for further details on the city and the university. Contact information For further information, please contact: Associate Professor, Yuya Hayashi ( [email protected] ). Deadline Applications must be received no later than 23 October 2026. Questions about the position\n                            ? Yuya Hayashi Associate Professor, Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Molecular Health +4587159712 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4593522463 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Wednesday 28 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-position-in-laboratory-astrochemistry-1": {
      "title": "Postdoc Position in Laboratory Astrochemistry",
      "description": "Postdoc Position in Laboratory Astrochemistry - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Physics and Astronomy Postdoc Position in Laboratory Astrochemistry Deadline 28 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Mar Department of Physics and Astronomy Ny Munkegade 120 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2029 ID: 170177 Apply The Aarhus Laboratory Astrochemistry Group is seeking applications for a postdoctoral position in laboratory astrochemistry under supervision of Prof. Liv Hornekær at the Department of Physics and Astronomy at Aarhus University. Job description The successful applicants will participate in the experimental research activities under ERC Synergy Grant IRAstro and DNRF Center of Excellence for Interstellar Catalysis – InterCat. This combined project aims at elucidating how molecules on interstellar dust grain analogue surfaces organize, react and dissipate energy under low temperature and low pressure conditions mimicking dense interstellar dust and molecular clouds. The applicant will use low temperature scanning tunneling microscopy (STM), temperature programmed desorption, and (single molecule) IR spectroscopy to follow in situ reactions leading to complex molecule formation on realistic dust grain analogue surfaces. Close collaboration with theoreticians, observers and the experimental astrochemistry groups at Aarhus University and at the nodes of collaborative partners is expected. The job includes: Mapping of catalytic pathways and reaction rates on interstellar dust grain analogue surfaces using STM, TPD, and RAIRS. development, maintenance and troubleshooting on various experimental UHV setups participating in maintenance of the group laboratories. data analysis and writing scientific publications. supervision of PhD, Master and bachelor students. project administration, such as writing reports etc. Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD degree in physics or similar field, with strong experience in experimental surface science, UHV techniques, catalysis/reactions on surfaces and atomic scale characterization. A background in laboratory astrochemistry is preferred. The ideal candidate has good collaboration skills and is also able to work independently. About IRAstro - Molecular Quantum Dynamics in Low-Temperature Astrochemistry The ERC Synergy project IRAstro brings together leading researchers in physics, chemistry, and astrochemistry to uncover how molecules behave and react under the extreme conditions of interstellar space. Recent observations from the James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) reveal the complexity of low temperature molecular ices frozen out on interstellar dust grains in regions where new stars and planetary systems are formed. Interpreting these observations requires a deeper understanding of the fundamental physical and chemical processes that govern molecular behaviour at temperatures close to absolute zero. IRAstro addresses this challenge by combining cutting-edge laboratory experiments, advanced instrumentation, and first-principles theory. The project focuses on the quantum dynamics of molecules in low-temperature condensed phases - a frontier area essential for understanding how chemical complexity arises in space. https://phys.au.dk/irastro About InterCat – Center for Interstellar Catalysis InterCat is a center of excellence supported by the Danish National Research Foundation. The center is situated at the Department of Physics and Astronomy and at the Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center at Aarhus University in Denmark, at Leiden Observatory at Leiden University in the Netherlands and Niels Bohr Institute at University of Copenhagen in Denmark. See https://phys.au.dk/intercat/ for further details. The scientific aim of the Center for Interstellar Catalysis is to determine whether the production of the molecular building blocks of life, e.g., sugars, amino acids, fats and DNA bases, predates the formation of stars and planets, and occurs at the extremely low temperatures and pressures found in interstellar space. InterCat thus aims to determine the molecular starting conditions for the origin of life across the universe. InterCat brings together research teams within surface science, nanoscience, heterogeneous catalysis and experimental and observational astrochemistry. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a 2-year postdoc position expected to start 1 March 2027 or as soon as possible. Contact information For further information, please contact: Prof. Liv Hornekær, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 28 October 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Liv Hornekær Professor, Department of Physics and Astronomy +4561663133 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520612514 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Wednesday 28 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/post-doc-in-plant-derived-extracellular-vesicles": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Post Doc in plant-derived extracellular vesicles - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Food Science - Differentiated & Biofunctional Foods - Faculty: Technical Sciences Post Doc in plant-derived extracellular vesicles Deadline 29 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Food Science - Differentiated & Biofunctional Foods Agro Food Park 48 8200 Aarhus N Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 170465 Apply Are you interested in extracellular vesicles from plants and their potential accumulation of unwanted compounds and are you willing to challenge conventional thinking? Then the Department of FOOD Science invites you to apply for a 2-year Post Doc position, funded under the Villum Experiment program. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a 2–year position from 1 January 2027, or as soon possible thereafter. Job description The position will focus on the isolation and characterization of extracellular vesicles from plants and understanding their cargo in relation to abiotic conditions. The Post Doc is expected to independently take part in cultivation of plants, optimizing methods for extraction of extracellular vesicles and development of targeted methods for assessing specific compounds. The Post Doc will likewise also be responsible for setting up and running bioactivity assays using mammalian cell-lines. Your profile Applicants should hold a PhD in biology, biochemistry, molecular biology, toxicology or similar. The preferred candidate has documented hands-on experience with extracellular vesicle isolation and characterization. Likewise hands-on experience with culturing of mammalian cells and various molecular techniques like qPCR, miRNA mapping and LC-MS, is considered positive. Strong collaborative skills, including experience working in interdisciplinary environments, are essential. We also value creative thinking, personal drive, and excellent communication skills. Who we are The Department of Food Science is part of the Faculty of Technical Sciences, Aarhus University and includes research within the areas Food Chemistry and Technology, Food Quality, Food Metabolomics and proteomics, Sensory Science, Biofunctional Food, Systems Biology, and Plant Production. The Department currently employs ca. 145 full-time staff, including 31 senior academic staff, ca. 60 PhD students and Postdocs, and ca. 45 technical and administrative staff. In addition, there are ca. 45 Bachelor and Master thesis students and ca. 10 visiting students and staff, who together contribute to an international research environment where English is the preferred language in the laboratory, at meetings and at seminars. The Department works in teams and therefore self-leadership, clear and open communication, success in mentoring, supervising and teaching are important qualities needed to be successful in the Department. In addition, the Department has a strong culture that is based on openness, equality, consultation, inclusion and contributing to the Department strategy and working environment are viewed as important. Aarhus University Food Science: Pioneering Research Partnerships What we offer The candidate will join the multidisciplinary research environment at AU FOOD, with activities spanning the entire food value chain from field to fork. The department provides access to state-of-the-art facilities for plant cultivation under controlled conditions, advanced instrumentation for biomolecular characterization, and two fully equipped cell culture laboratories. The candidate will join an international and multicultural workplace where English is the primary working language. The department also offers a variety of social activities that contribute to a welcoming and inclusive environment. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Agro Food Park 48, 8200 Aarhus N, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. Contact information For further information, please contact: Associated Professor, Martin Krøyer Rasmussen, +452216 9390, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 29 October 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Martin Krøyer Rasmussen Associate Professor, Department of Food Science - Differentiated & Biofunctional Foods +4522169390 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday 29 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
