    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/academic-position-as-beamline-scientist-within-micro-and-nano-focused-photoemission-spectroscopy": {
      "title": "Academic position as beamline specialist within micro- and nano-focused photoemission spectroscopy",
      "description": "Academic position as beamline specialist within micro- and nano-focused photoemission spectroscopy - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Physics and Astronomy Academic position as beamline specialist within micro- and nano-focused photoemission spectroscopy Deadline 2 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Feb Department of Physics and Astronomy Ny Munkegade 120 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 170413 Apply The Department of Physics and Astronomy, Aarhus University, Denmark invites applications for an Academic employee position as beamline specialist at the ASTRID2 light source with expected start date on 1 February 2027. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a permanent position starting from 1 February 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description You will run and maintain ultra-high vacuum (UHV) beamlines at the ASTRID2 light source in collaboration with the Institute of Storage Ring Facilities (ISA). You will be the main responsible person of maintaining the AU-SGM4 beamline and end-station infrastructure for micro- and nano-focused angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (nanoARPES) experiments, as well as contributing to the future developments of this capability You will contribute to user support and coordination of both internal and external users (including students doing projects at the beamline) during initial contact and feasibility discussions, during beamtimes and following up on data handling practices and analysis. You will be involved in commissioning tasks such as integration of the new APPLE-II undulator for polarization-resolved experiments You will help with infrastructure adjustments in general relating to the ARPES system itself and the connected systems, which include a glovebox, closed-cycle cryo cooler, UHV preparation chambers as well as a PHAROS Light Conversion femtosecond laser system. You will lead the development of control and analysis software solutions in relation to the above activities Your profile Applicants must hold a master in Physics or similar and are expected to document experience with angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy or similar techniques to probe excitations in solids use of synchrotron beamline facilities to investigate properties of matter a track record of designing, maintaining and applying UHV systems in the context of experimental physics handling of quantum materials, 2D materials or other types of solid-state systems in advanced modalities such as prototypical device architectures Experiences with nanoscale angle-resolved photoemission (nanoARPES), femtosecond time-resolved (TR-)ARPES and with analysis of photoemission data resulting from such experiments are considered major advantages. A PhD in Physics is also considered an advantage. The applicant is expected to be a team-player that is strongly motivated towards collaboration and able to compassionately work with and train students and users. Who we are The Department of Physics and Astronomy is a department at the Faculty of Natural Sciences, Aarhus University, Denmark. The main objectives of the Department are to carry out research at the highest international level, to offer research-based teaching at Bachelor of Science, Master of Science and PhD levels, and to exchange knowledge with other areas of society, e.g. by offering our expertise to industrial partners and the business world. The Department is an attractive workplace for researchers, teachers, technicians, laboratory technicians and administrators and has a good study environment with many talented and active students at all levels. The research at the Department of Physics and Astronomy is of high quality, attracts significant international attention – in several fields the Department is within the world elite – and is characterized by many international collaborations with other high-ranking universities and research institutions around the world. The Department uniquely contains the ASTRID2 light source, which is a third generation 580 MeV synchrotron running in top-up mode (see https://www.isa.au.dk/index.asp). The synchrotron consists of 6 beamlines that typically cover research themes such as atomic and molecular physics, catalysis, astrochemistry, surface science and quantum materials, giving rise to a highly collaborative, diverse and stimulating research environment. What we offer The successful candidate is offered: a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national and international collaborators a work environment encouraging lively, open and critical discussion a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Ny Munkegade 120, 8000 Aarhus C and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments Contact information For further information, please contact: Associate Professor, Søren Ulstrup, [email protected] Deadline Applications must be received no later than 2 November 2026. Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Søren Ulstrup Associate Professor, Department of Physics and Astronomy +4587150512 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522521469 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday  2 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/assistant-professor-of-materials-science-engineering-up-to-4-positions": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Materials Science & Engineering (up to 4 positions)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Materials Science & Engineering (up to 4 positions) - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering - Functional Materials - Faculty: Technical Sciences Assistant Professor of Materials Science & Engineering (up to 4 positions) Deadline 3 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering - Functional Materials Åbogade 40A 8200 Aarhus N Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2030 ID: 170539 Apply The Materials Science & Engineering Center at the Department of Biological & Chemical Engineering, Aarhus University, Denmark, invites applications for Assistant Professors. The successful candidate(s) will be affiliated with the Center’s projects led by Prof. Dr. Anja-Verena Mudring and funded by the NovoNordisk Foundation. Expected start date and duration of employment The positions are 4-year positions from 1 January 2027 or as soon possible. Your job responsibilities As an Assistant Professor of Materials Science & Engineering at our center, you are expected to conduct research at a high international level in the center’s projects under the guidance of Prof. Anja-Verena Mudring. You are committed to delivering high-quality teaching and supervision and bring collaborative skills, and intellectual curiosity. You are eager to engage in the academic community at Aarhus University and contribute to strengthening the department's national and international profile. We also value academic citizenship. This includes active participation in collegial collaboration, mentoring, and the social and professional life of the unit. We therefore expect you to be an engaged and visible member of the unit's daily academic environment. Your main tasks will consist of: Research of high international quality in the center projects Disseminate research results in scientific articles and contributions at international congresses. Contribution towards attracting external research funding. Teaching and supervising students and interns. You will report to the Head of the Materials Science & Engineering Center Prof. Dr. Anja-Verena Mudring. Your competences PhD in Materials Science, Material Engineering, Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Physics or equivalent Postdoctoral experience in a field of materials science & engineering Thorough experience in one or more of the following - in solid state synthesis like synthesis of molten salt compounds, alloys and intermetallic compounds - synthesis of molten salts and ionic liquids - synthesis of inorganic and hybrid materials using molten salts and ionic liquids - corrosion of metals/reaction of molten salts/ionic liquids with metals and alloys - f-element chemistry - materials characterization with X-ray and/or electron and/or neutron diffraction methods - characterization of thermal materials properties - characterization of magnetic and/or mechanical properties of materials Scientific publications in international peer-reviewed journals in the area of materials science as first author and co-author Fluent in oral and written English. As a person, you are motivated, ambitious, take ownership and possess good communication and interpersonal skills. Who we are The Materials Science and Engineering Center at the Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering at Aarhus University, Denmark is a unit that is comprising materials synthesis, characterization and modelling. Its current focus is the investigation of ionic liquids and high temperature molten salts for engineering applications such as thermal energy storage and management, lubrication, molten salt reactor research as well as task-specific media for active pharmaceutical ingredient development. What we offer The centre offers: a well-developed research infrastructure an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Åbogade 40, 8200 Aarhus N, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact Professor Anja-Verena Mudring ( [email protected] ). Deadline Applications must be received no later than 3 November 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities. Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Anja Verena Mudring Professor, Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering - Functional Materials [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520612514 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday  3 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/laboratory-and-field-technician-engineer-in-mechatronics-at-land-craft-2": {
      "title": "Laboratory and field technician/engineer in mechatronics",
      "description": "Laboratory and field technician/engineer in mechatronics at Land-CRAFT  - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Agroecology - Center for Landscape Research in Sustainable Agricultural Futures - Land-CRAFT Laboratory and field technician/engineer in mechatronics at Land-CRAFT Deadline 5 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Agroecology - Center for Landscape Research in Sustainable Agricultural Futures - Land-CRAFT Ole Worms Allé 3 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2029 ID: 170453 Apply The Pioneer Center for Landscape Research in Sustainable Agricultural Futures, Land-CRAFT , at the Department of Agroecology at Aarhus University, Denmark, is offering a technical position starting on 01-01-2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is for 3 years. At Land-CRAFT we make custom tools and measurement devices to support our research projects. We measure mostly soil greenhouse gas emissions, and operate systems with gas chambers, sensors, mesocosms and more. Food security, climate change and loss of biodiversity represent three of today’s major societal challenges. Finding solutions for all these challenges requires studies that extend across multiple scales. The Pioneer Center Land-CRAFT was established in June 2022 to undertake fundamental and applied research from field to landscape scales that will address these societal challenges. Digital technologies, including modelling and remote sensing, are key components of the Center. We have a variety of activities in the field, in our laboratories, and in the workshop as an integrated part of our research with the above-mentioned goals. There is always room for creativity and idea exchange within the group, and it is important for us that everyone enjoys their work, while delivering the required solutions. We need a new team member for our technical team running our field and lab experiments. You will be part of an international research environment and refer to the Center Director Prof. Klaus Butterbach-Bahl. Job description Your main tasks are: Supporting early career scientists in running laboratory and field experiments Building/designing innovative custom-made measurement systems for quantifying soil greenhouse gas emissions Identifying and purchasing lab and field equipment like environmental sensors for soil-environmental conditions, laser spectroscopes for trace-gas analysis, etc. Coordinating the maintenance of the equipment Supporting the measurements of Nitrogen balance and soil greenhouse gas fluxes at, among other sites, SmartField site) Your profile We expect that you are an engineer in mechatronics, mechanical/electrical design or similar have experience with connecting mechanical equipment to digital systems, as well as assuring continuous data retrieval have experience with a wide range of sensor technologies and how to develop and program environmental sensor networks have good English and Danish skills (The language of research at the Center is English, while Danish will be needed to interact with farmers, local shops, etc.) are motivated by working on projects, where overall aim is to support the green transition in agriculture can work independently have a driver’s license, as you will need to visit the various experimental sites experience with gas analysers is a plus Who we are At the Department of Agroecology our main goal is to contribute to sustainable solutions to some of the world’s biggest problems within the areas of soil, plants, animals, humans and the environment. We want to make a difference by contributing to both fundamental knowledge generation and the attainment of sustainable production systems via innovative research, contracted policy advice and education. We offer professional laboratories, greenhouses, semi-field and field-scale research facilities, advanced computing capacities as well as an extensive national and international researcher network. The Department consists of nine research sections with around 360 highly dedicated employees of which approximately 50% are scientific staff. More information can be found here . What we offer A collaborative, international research environment that combines high academic standards with an informal and supportive atmosphere. We value accountability, curiosity, flexibility, and teamwork in everything we do. An inclusive and respectful workplace culture, where mutual trust, kindness, and professional dialogue are part of daily life. We encourage open communication and develop a cohesive sense of community across teams and disciplines. A flexible working environment that supports work-life balance and individual needs. An active institutional commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion – in recruitment, career development, and everyday interactions. An innovative and meaningful workplace where your work contributes to solving real-world challenges. No two days are alike, and we welcome creative thinking and new ideas. Living and working in Denmark Subsidised childcare and free education from primary school through university. Universal healthcare for you and your family as residents. Five weeks of paid holiday per year. Generous parental leave – up to 52 weeks shared between parents, with full or partial salary. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a full-time position (37 hours/week) and available from 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Duration of employment: 3 years. Deadline for application is 05. November 2026. The main place of work is Ole Worms Alle 3, 8000, Aarhus C, while field experiments might vary in location (national and international). Applicants seeking further information can contact Professor Klaus Butterbach-Bahl (e-mail: [email protected] ) Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between: Tthe Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions, where you will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8 The Danish Ministry of Taxation and HK/Stat for clerical staff, laboratory technicians and IT staff The Danish Ministry of Taxationand the Teachers' Central Organisation and CO10 – The Central Organisation of 2010 (The LC/CO10 collective agreement) and the organizational agreement for agricultural technicians/agricultural technologists in the state sector as well as research technicians at Aarhus University, where you will be employed as an agricultural technologist/agricultural technician with terms of salary and employment in accordance with the Circular on the organizational agreement for agricultural technologists/agricultural technicians in the state sector as well as research technicians at Aarhus University ( https://cirkulaere.medst.dk/love-regler-og-aftaler/cirkulaerer/2025/april/048-25-cirkulaere-om-organisationsaftale-for-jordbrugsteknologerjordbrugsteknikere-i-staten-samt-forsoegsteknikere-ved-aarhus-universitet/ ). Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Klaus Butterbach-Bahl Centre Director, Professor, Department of Agroecology - Center for Landscape Research in Sustainable Agricultural Futures - Land-CRAFT +4593508238 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522140468 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday  5 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/assistant-professor-in-plant-microbe-interactions": {
      "title": "Assistant professor in plant-microbe interactions",
      "description": "Assistant professor in plant-microbe interactions - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Agroecology - Plant Pathology and Microbiology - Faculty: Technical Sciences Assistant professor in plant-microbe interactions Deadline 5 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Mar Department of Agroecology - Plant Pathology and Microbiology Forsøgsvej 1 4200 Slagelse Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2030 ID: 170551 Apply The Department of Agroecology at Aarhus University, Denmark, is offering a position as Assistant professor focusing on interactions between the barley genome and root microbial assembly. The position will be available from 01-03-2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position will be available for a 3-year period. You will be part of a research environment focusing on plant-microbe interactions and microbial ecology. You will be contributing to specifically the area of barley genomic effects on microbial composition and function of roots. And the main focus of your position will be to study barley drought resilience and how it arises from plant-microbe interactions at gene-for-gene level. You will integrate plant phenotypic data, large-scale root microbiome metabarcoding data with barley SNP and whole-genome data, as well as culturomics and microbial genomic data. You will design and conduct controlled experiments, including in-vitro assays and experiments using a high-throughput plant phenotyping platform. The project aims to elucidate plant genetic and microbial genomic interactions determining microbiome-mediated drought protection in barley. We expect that you will be an important part of the research environment and that you will contribute positively to the social working environment. We also expect that you will take part in our teaching activities and that you will report research results in high-impact scientific journals. Your profile We are searching for a highly motivated candidate who has A PhD in plant-microbe interactions, plant microbial ecology or similar Collaborative skills and ability to participate actively in teams A genuine interest in classical as well as molecular microbiology Documented experience in scientific writing and publication in peer-reviewed scientific journals Teaching and supervision experience at BSc, MSc and PhD level Experience in writing research proposals for external funding Research experience in some of the areas of plant genomics and microbiome analysis. Networking experience Additional qualifications Further, we will prefer candidates with some of the following qualifications: Experience in planning and conducting laboratory work with plants, soils and microbiomes. Experience in analysis of microbiome and genome data. Insight into plant and microbial genomics Experience with statistical methods, bioinformatics and reproducible computational workflows. Who we are At the Department of Agroecology, our main goal is to contribute to sustainable solutions to some of the world’s biggest problems within the areas of soil, plants, animals, humans, and the environment. We want to make a difference by contributing to both fundamental knowledge generation and the attainment of sustainable production systems via innovative research, contracted policy advice, and education. We offer professional laboratories, greenhouses, semi-field, and field-scale research facilities, advanced computing capacities as well as an extensive national and international researcher network. The department consists of nine research sections with around 350 highly skilled employees, of which approximately 50% are scientific staff. More information can be found here . We believe in encouraging inclusion, acceptance, and understanding by employing staff who bring unique perspectives to our department. What we offer A collaborative, international research environment that combines high academic standards with an informal and supportive atmosphere. We value accountability, curiosity, flexibility, and teamwork in everything we do. An inclusive and respectful workplace culture, where mutual trust, kindness, and professional dialogue are part of daily life. We encourage open communication and develop a cohesive sense of community across teams and disciplines. A flexible working environment that supports work-life balance and individual needs. An active institutional commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion – in recruitment, career development, and everyday interactions. An innovative and meaningful workplace where your work contributes to solving real-world challenges. No two days are alike, and we welcome creative thinking and new ideas. Support for international researchers and their families, including Relocation Service and an Expat Partner Programme Living and working in Denmark Subsidised childcare and free education from primary school through university. Universal healthcare for you and your family as residents. Five weeks of paid holiday per year. Generous parental leave – up to 52 weeks shared between parents, with full or partial salary. Place of Work The place of work is Aarhus University, Campus Flakkebjerg (4200 Slagelse, Denmark). The affiliation will be with the Department of Agroecology. More information can be obtained from Prof. Mogens Nicolaisen ( [email protected] ). Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities. Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Jørgen Eivind Olesen Head of Department, Department of Agroecology +4540821659 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520968189 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday  5 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/project-manager-for-international-research-projects": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Project manager for international research projects - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering - Partnerships and external relations, Gustav Wieds Vej 10D Project manager for international research projects Deadline 9 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering - Partnerships and external relations, Gustav Wieds Vej 10D Gustav Wieds Vej 10D 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 170550 Apply Are you enthusiastic about making a difference to large societal challenges such as green transition of energy, materials and food systems? Do you have experience with research project management, funding administration and project development? Do you know your way around a Horizon Europe project and do you enjoy being the person who keeps a large international consortium on track? Do you thrive in a dynamic working environment, with multiple external and internal stakeholders? Then we might be looking for you! At the Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering at Aarhus University, we are busy conducting research on a broad range of topics related to green transition, sustainability and health. Our portfolio of EU-funded projects is growing, and we are therefore looking for an experienced EU project manager with international experience who can drive development and execution of international projects in close collaboration with researchers, external partners and the department’s Partnerships and External Relations team. Expected starting date and duration The position is a two-year fixed-term position starting on 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job descripton At Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering, we are experiencing an increase in externally funded, international research and innovation projects. As project manager you will play a key role in managing and coordinating the department’s participation in such projects, in particular large Horizon Europe projects with multiple partners, where our department has a leading role Working closely with the scientific coordinator, you will: Act as the day-to-day operational point of contact for the consortium. Drive the coordination machinery: Track deliverables, milestones and risks, chase and quality-assure partner contributions. Prepare periodic/final reporting, and coordinate financial reporting with partners and with the administrative and financial experts in AU’s central research support office. Plan and run consortium, General Assembly and review meetings: Agendas, minutes and follow-up on decisions. Maintain the Consortium Agreement and support obligations on data management, ethics, dissemination, exploitation and communication. In addition to this, you will: Support the department’s wider portfolio of international projects, being work package or task manager where relevant. Provide guidance and support to the development of new European grant proposals and the building of new consortia. Participate in and help organise externally oriented activities and events. Help shape the department’s way of working around externally funded projects, so that good practice is developed and shared. The role is vested in the department’s Partnerships and External Relations team. The team supports our researchers within multiple aspects of externally funded research collaborations - from strategy development, visibility, partnership and project development to execution of complex projects. We are continuously developing our way of working to support increased activity level and creation of impact. Qualifications We are looking for someone who combines hands-on experience of EU project execution with the ability to work comfortably alongside researchers in a technical field. Technical qualifications: Academic degree in a relevant field (e.g., technical sciences, international relations etc.). Experience with the day-to-day management of Horizon Europe or Horizon 2020 projects; including reporting, deliverable and milestone tracking. Experience of working with international research consortia and of coordinating contributions from partners in several countries. The ability to engage with technical content well enough to quality-assure deliverables and to discuss the work with researchers on their own terms. Qualifications we regard as an advantage: Experience from the coordinator side of an EU project, rather than as a beneficiary partner only. Experience with proposal development, grant writing and consortium building. Familiarity with research areas related to the green transition; for example bioresources, biotechnology, chemical or environmental engineering. Familiarity with EU financial rules, cost eligibility and audit requirements. Personal qualifications: Excellent communication skills, with fluency in English. Knowledge of Danish is an advantage. Strong organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple stakeholders and priorities effectively Service-minded attitude, flexibility, and a collaborative approach to working in a team Ability to travel internationally as needed (few times per year). Who we are At the Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering we focus on living systems, the biology of organisms, efficient production, and transformation of chemicals, materials and energy. We translate knowledge within biotechnology, food technology, environmental technology, chemical engineering, industrial biotechnology, medical biotechnology and electrochemical engineering etc. more into new technologies and value-creating solutions that can also be used in the business community. Many of our research and development activities are based on companies' specific innovation needs or specialist application areas. Close collaboration with the public sector and private businesses ensures that the knowledge and technology generated in the department's research environments has a clear anchoring in reality and benefits the surrounding society. We also provide research-based public sector consultancy and advice on technology in agriculture and food. At the department, we educate BEng, BSc and MSc graduates in engineering for Danish society. Read more about the department and sections here: https://bce.au.dk/en/research/key-areas-in-research-and-development Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering offers: Opportunity to contribute to solutions of crucial importance to society and industry The possibility of being part of a dynamic research environment in rapid development An exciting, cross-disciplinary environment with strong external partnerships with industrial and academic partners in Denmark and abroad A workplace characterized by professionalism, trust and healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of work Gustav Wieds Vej 10D, 8000 Aarhus, Denmark and the area of employment is Aarhus University, Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering. Contact information For further information, please contact: Deputy head of department, Thomas Lundgaard, +45 29 16 31 35 / [email protected] Application deadline 9 November 2026. Formalities and salary range You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. For the right candidate, there will be an opportunity to be appointed as a Special Consultant, with remuneration in accordance with Appendix 4 of the collective agreement. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Thomas Lundgaard Deputy Head of Department, Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering - Partnerships and external relations, Gustav Wieds Vej 10D +4529163135 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522521469 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday  9 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/special-call-phd-scholarship-on-social-acceleration-and-democratic-lawmaking": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Special Call PhD scholarship on social acceleration and democratic lawmaking - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Political Science Special Call PhD scholarship on social acceleration and democratic lawmaking Deadline 16 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Feb Department of Political Science Bartholins Allé 7 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Feb 2027 - 31 Jan 2030 ID: 169939 Apply We invite applications for a PhD scholarship in political science focusing on the relationship between social acceleration and democratic lawmaking. The successful candidate will join the ERC-funded SLOMODEMO project and contribute to cutting-edge research on democracy in an accelerating society. The overall research project The PhD scholarship is part of SLOMODEMO (High Speed Society, Slow Motion Democracy, and the Dilemma for Democratic Problem Solving), a research project funded by the European Research Council and led by Professor Kees van Kersbergen. SLOMODEMO investigates how liberal democracies respond when technological change, social change, and the pace of everyday life accelerate, while democratic procedures remain intentionally deliberative, rule-bound, and comparatively slow. The project reconceptualizes liberal democracy as a problem-solving system whose legitimacy depends on both democratic procedures and the capacity to address collective problems effectively and in time. The project combines theory development with comparative measurement, large-scale data collection, observational analysis, surveys, and experiments. It covers Denmark, Estonia, Germany, India, the Netherlands, Switzerland, the United Kingdom, and the United States, primarily from the 1990s onward. The PhD project will contribute to one of the work packages (WP2, project 2.2, available here ), which examines the impact of social acceleration on democratic lawmaking. The research group The PhD student will become a member of the SLOMODEMO research group at the Department of Political Science, Aarhus University. The group brings together researchers working on social acceleration, democratic problem solving, political and legal institutions, public opinion, survey experiments, and computational social science. The PhD student will work closely with the principal investigator and the other researchers in SLOMODEMO, contribute to common project activities and seminars, and take part in the wider research environment of the department. Job description The overall research question for the PhD study is: To what extent, under what conditions, and how does social acceleration affect democratic lawmaking? Applicants are invited to formulate a focused and original PhD project within this broad framework. Themes of particular relevance include the capacity of legislative institutions to keep pace with changing societal demands; institutional variation in legislative adaptation; attempts by political actors to accelerate or reorganize lawmaking; and the consequences of such developments for deliberation, accountability, and democratic problem-solving capacity. These themes indicate the intellectual scope of the PhD project but do not prescribe particular sub-research questions or research designs. Applicants are expected to formulate and justify their own focused research questions. The proposed project must have a clear comparative and empirical orientation and should connect meaningfully to SLOMODEMO’s countries, period, concepts, or data infrastructure. Applicants are free to propose the theoretical argument, research design, data sources, measurements, and analytical methods best suited to their research questions, provided that the project is feasible within a PhD programme of three years and contributes to the objectives of the Work Package (WP2, project 2.2, available here ). Potential empirical materials include legislative and institutional data, legal texts, parliamentary or administrative documents, and other sources suitable for tracing the pace, content, or organization of lawmaking over time. Relevant approaches include longitudinal quantitative analysis, comparative analysis, automated data collection, and computational text analysis. Applicants should justify their methodological choices rather than attempt to include all these elements. The PhD project is to be carried out as part of the research project. The PhD student´s role in the research questions listed above will depend on the chosen candidate and his / her own PhD project description related to the topic of the overall project. Hence, you must write a project description on how you would carry out the research within the frames of the overall project. During the project you will complete a PhD education as described in the rules and regulations for the PhD programme . Your qualifications Applicants must have: A relevant master’s degree in political science, public policy, sociology, computational social science, data science, or a closely related field, completed before enrolment. A foundation in quantitative social-science research design and comparative political analysis. Demonstrable proficiency in R or a comparable programming environment and experience with transparent and reproducible data analysis. The ability to formulate and carry out an independent PhD project while collaborating closely in a larger research team. Excellent written and spoken English. Documented experience with one or more of the following will be an advantage: Longitudinal, multilevel, Bayesian, or other advanced quantitative methods relevant to comparative research. Measurement of complex political or social concepts and analysis of change over time. Automated and responsible data collection from databases, APIs, and web sources. Computational analysis of multilingual political or legal documents, including machine translation, supervised classification, or validated use of large language models. Comparative research on democratic institutions, legislative politics, public policy, political time, or social acceleration. Open-science practices, including transparent documentation, reusable code, validation procedures, and reproducible research outputs. No applicant is expected to possess all the listed methodological competencies. Familiarity with one or more of the project’s national or legal contexts is an advantage but not required. The Department and PhD programme The Department of Political Science at Aarhus University is an internationally oriented research department with strong environments in comparative politics, political institutions, public policy, political behaviour, and advanced quantitative methods. The PhD student will be affiliated with the relevant section and be part of the PhD programme for political science, and will as such follow the ordinary rules and regulations for the programme and have access to the department's seminars, methods training, international networks, and collaborative research activities. Further information For further information about the position, please contact Professor Kees van Kersbergen, [email protected] . If you need help uploading your application or have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR Coordinator Mette Fisker Præstegaard, [email protected] . Place of work Department of Political Science, Bartholins Allé 7, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. What do we offer? Aarhus BSS is part of Aarhus University, an internationally recognised research university with well-established academic environments and close engagement with society and industry. As a PhD student, you will gain: access to renowned researchers and international networks an engaging and supportive research environment a pathway to a research career or a career in the private or public sector We are committed to creating an inclusive environment and recognize diversity as a key asset. We encourage all qualified candidates to apply. Assessment Criteria Applications are assessed based on an overall evaluation of the applicant’s qualifications. Particular emphasis is placed on: the quality, originality, and relevance of the research proposal academic performance in both bachelor’s and master’s degree studies The final decision regarding admission is made by the management based on an assessment of both the quality of the proposed project and the applicant’s ability to complete it within the prescribed timeframe. Applicants are encouraged to include documentation of their class ranking (e.g. ranking or percentile), as this provides additional context for the assessment. However, this is not a requirement. Applicants should document relevant competencies through their master thesis, course papers, research-assistant work, software or code repositories, replication materials, or comparable outputs. Framework for the PhD Programme The PhD programme is conducted in accordance with the current Danish Ministerial Order on the PhD Programme and the rules and guidelines for PhD students at Aarhus BSS, both of which can be found here . It is not essential that your educational background matches the PhD programme exactly. The most important consideration is that your project is academically relevant and can be anchored within the research area of the PhD programme. How to apply You can read more about the application requirements in the application guide and find answers to frequently asked questions concerning the application process in our FAQ . Terms of Employment If admitted to the 3-year PhD programme, you will also be offered employment in accordance with the Ministerial Order on the Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities (in Danish) and the collective agreement for academics employed by the Danish state (in Danish). The job description and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities. Salary and employment terms are governed by the collective agreement for academics employed by the Danish state . You will be appointed as a PhD fellow at base pay level 4–8, depending on your educational background and qualifications. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Aarhus University Aarhus University is an academically diverse and research-intensive university with a strong commitment to high-quality research and education and the development of society nationally and globally. The university offers an inspiring research and teaching environment to its 37,000 students (FTEs) and 8,700 employees and has an annual revenue of EUR 1.106 billion. Learn more at www.international.au.dk/ Questions about the position\n                            ? Kees van Kersbergen Professor, Department of Political Science +4587150648 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Aarhus BSS Administrative Centre - Aarhus BSS HR and PhD Administration +4593522807 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 16 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
